## Runtime-upgrade invariants

A runtime contract is host control-plane evidence, not Pi conversation state. It
may advance only after the guest answers the room-ID SSH probe. `room.json`
`last_attempted_revision` can move during preparation; `runtime_contract` and
`last_revision` move only at readiness.

The active slot is `ROOM/upgrades/active/` and contains only a fixed manifest,
`rollback.raw`, optional exact `room-before.json`, and—during/after rollback—
`forward.raw` plus optional exact `room-forward.json`. Never add workspace,
credentials, host Nix objects, VMM RAM/device state, exported Pi transcript data,
or arbitrary diagnostics. The disk is opaque: native Pi sessions, installed
packages, MCP settings and guest Nix state already inside it move with whichever
whole disk is current. The rollback disk must remain the same logical capacity as
the current disk; structural mutations are therefore blocked until the slot is
accepted or cleared.

Publication order is part of the contract. Prepare into a private staging
directory, fsync, then rename to `active`. Rollback first commits the exact target
metadata preimage, records intent, moves the current disk to `forward.raw`, moves
`rollback.raw` to `root.raw`, restores the exact source metadata preimage, then
publishes `rolled-back` and a receipt. A rollback whose target reached readiness
may run the inverse `forward` transaction and restore the exact target metadata.
Every intermediate stage/layout in both directions must be replayable. A stale
stage with moved disk files must be recognized as recovery work and must never
boot.

A ready slot is a convenience rollback point, not an indefinite generation
history. One newer runtime may supersede it by accepting it first. Any failed or
rolled-back slot must remain explicit. Accepting a completed rollback or retrying
may discard the retained newer disk only through an explicit confirmed command.
Do not auto-retry a guest request, infer upgrade success from process launch, or
silently discard either side of the disk switch.

Cloud Hypervisor RAM snapshots are deliberately outside this mechanism. Upstream
restore compatibility is tied to the VMM version; Sandpi's cross-revision object
is the cold raw guest disk plus exact bounded host metadata.


## Store and checkpoint invariants

Host and guest roots preserve the cumulative lower lease for every surviving
room/checkpoint. No --realise on unrelated .drv paths just to enumerate a cache.
Use registration records, never copy a running SQLite Nix database.

`storeview.py` runs only in the share backend's single-user root-mapped mount
namespace. It bind-mounts leased directory/file objects, recreates Nix symlinks,
then makes the synthetic root read-only. `--announce-submounts` matters. Check
mount count/startup cost at realistic host-store sizes. A fresh state root with
--store-cache base limits additions; switching an existing one does not prune it.

Overlay index/metacopy/xino are off and directory redirects not followed to
avoid retaining a prior synthetic lower identity across boots. Stop rooms before
in-place repair of leased Nix objects. The guest can install/build Nix packages;
nixos-rebuild inside it does not change the host-selected next boot kernel.

Checkpoints are offline disk-only, not CH RAM state, not portable backups and not
workspace snapshots. Root and workdir stay on persistent ext4. A safety copy is
retained before restore; a crash between swap and receipt completion is diagnosable.
Actual root.raw capacity wins over stale metadata. Sparse capacity is not reserved
host storage, and reflink block counts are not unique physical allocation.

Storage retirement is host policy. `clean --room` must not delete live workspace
files or credentials. `clean --cache` is valid only after every room/checkpoint and
quarantined disk is gone; it releases Sandpi's lower-store roots but does not run
Nix GC. An interrupted deletion remains in `root/trash` until explicit review.

