# sandpi — rev0086 / Orbit

**An agent-owned NixOS machine, mostly stock Pi, and a small host launcher.**

The executable `sandpi` is a standalone Bash file containing the readable Python,
Bash, TypeScript, Nix, documentation and test sources. Nix supplies the actual Pi,
Cloud Hypervisor and system dependencies. No project data or credentials are baked
into a Nix derivation.

## This revision — bounded autonomous work in native Pi

Orbit adds a Pi-native work loop without creating a second conversation engine:

```text
/loop "do this foobar"
/loop 12 "finish the refactor, verify it, and red-team the result"
/loop status
/loop stop
```

The default is five complete Pi agent settlements; an explicit count may be
1–100. A settlement can contain multiple provider requests for tool use, retry or
compaction, so the count is not a request or spending cap. Each continuation stays
inside the current native conversation and preserves the selected model, thinking,
tools, billing permission and provider behavior.

Escape, a provider error, a rejected final continuation, session movement or
process exit closes loop authority. `/loop stop` lets the current pass settle
but schedules no next loop pass. Restarted sessions show stale running state as
interrupted and never resume it automatically. See
[native Pi work loops](docs/PI-WORK-LOOP.md) and
[the release report](docs/RELEASE-0.86.0.md).

## Rev0085 — keep long sessions routed and copyable

Longhaul repairs defects exposed by an ordinary paid DeepSeek session in a
long-lived TriKEM room. OpenRouter account metadata was refreshed only at boot;
after its ten-minute TTL, paid routing forgot which key belonged to the paid
account and selected a known free-tier key. The model had successfully completed
the project's quick test suite before the following request failed.

Account metadata now stays warm for the credential server's whole lifetime.
Current paid evidence comes first, followed by bounded last-known paid evidence,
unknown state, and negative evidence. A transient metadata failure no longer
erases the classification, freshness remains visible, and every otherwise healthy
key remains fallback-eligible.

Sandpi also defaults Pi to regular TUI mode so the caller's terminal owns
scrollback and highlighted-text copying across the VM transport. Saved user
choices and explicit fullscreen requests still win; native `/copy` remains
available for the last assistant response.

Kestrel's verified repeat-boot acceleration remains intact. The provider boundary
and pinned Pi 1.1.0 are unchanged. See [the release report](docs/RELEASE-0.85.0.md)
for the reproduction, safety boundaries, and verification evidence. Switchyard's
ten-model matrix remains in [its release report](docs/RELEASE-0.84.0.md).

## Rev0081 — focus one local catalog delta on exact IDs

```bash
sandpi models --diff previous current --focus vendor/model
sandpi models --diff previous current \
  --focus vendor/model-a \
  --focus vendor/model-b
sandpi models --paid --diff previous current --focus vendor/paid-model --json
```

Waypoint keeps Tidemark's complete exact-ID delta and adds a repeatable,
invocation-local focus query. Each requested ID is reported as added, removed,
metadata-changed, unchanged or absent from both snapshots. That distinction cannot
be recovered by merely filtering rev0080's changed-row JSON because unchanged rows
were intentionally absent.

Human output retains the whole-pair counts but shows only focused IDs. Structured
output keeps the complete ordinary delta and adds bounded before/after evidence for
each requested ID. The query is exact, case-sensitive, limited to 32 unique IDs,
and never saved or monitored.

Inspection remains local unless `--refresh` is explicitly supplied. It does not
resolve aliases, infer successors, select a model, enable payment, probe provider
readiness, or mutate a running Pi process. See
[exact-ID catalog focus](docs/PI-CATALOG-FOCUS.md),
[research](docs/RESEARCH-REV0081.md), and fresh receipts in `evidence/rev0081/`.
Live refresh, installed-Pi re-entry, provider/account, and Nix/SSH/VM acceptance
remain unproven here.

