Sandpi 0.41.0 / Tariffglass, reviewed 2026-10-07.

## What changes quickly, and what should not

A model recommendation is not a security decision, benchmark certificate,
credential grant, conversation format, or provider protocol. This revision keeps
those concerns separate instead of spreading vendor names through the launcher.

| Layer | Source | Responsibility |
|---|---|---|
| Model opinions | `src/pi/model-defaults.json` | Exact preset IDs, starting thinking, additive tool profile, output ceiling, rationale, references, review window |
| Opinion validation | `src/model_defaults.py` | Bounded schema, exact matching, expiry and Pi-version checks; no inference, credentials or VM operations |
| Defaults ownership | `src/settings_defaults.py` | Pure field-wise reconciliation of advisory personal settings; no project/session/network parsing |
| Factual model metadata | `src/catalog.py` | Live pricing class, capabilities, lifecycle, context and native API; independent free and all-model cache views |
| Pi integration | `src/guest.py`, `src/pi/index.ts` | Compose current recommendations with user settings; diagnostics and native lifecycle hooks |
| Request/account policy | `src/pi/pool.mjs`, `src/keypool.py` | Explicit paid admission, exact routing, usage preservation, key scheduling, scoped failure and no replay |

The first row is intentionally easy to discard. Empty `recipes: []` disables all
model-specific recommendations. A missing file is equivalent to no active
opinions; a malformed file fails with a repairable configuration error. Removing
the table does not remove paid support, rewrite credentials, erase sessions,
change endpoint transports or import old small-model grades into new models.

## Recipe rules

Every row names one exact OpenRouter ID, the exact `canonical_slug` reviewed
under that alias, one unique preset, a descriptive role, an additive profile, an
output ceiling and a thinking preference. No globs, vendor-wide promotions, size
thresholds or name-based claims of intelligence. Current recipes are reviewed for
Pi 1.1.0 through November 6, 2026. The loader bounds a review window to 90 days;
the checked-in window is 30 days.

A recommendation applies only to a priced, tool-capable, active catalog model
within that window and on a reviewed Pi version. The live alias must still name
the reviewed canonical revision. Expired, missing, or moved opinions fall back to
native/generic defaults; they do not ban the model. A preset never follows a
successor. Use `sandpi presets --refresh` for an inference-free live audit.

`thinking: native` means no invented effort preference. Another value is used
only when live catalog evidence supports it. User edits to per-model thinking or
compaction settings cause the existing overlay to relinquish those values. The
code never treats a recipe as authority to strip or recreate signed reasoning.

Codemode recommendations add or manage that one presentation option. They do not
own all direct/MCP tools. Raw native tool flags or passthrough override managed
presentation; an explicit profile remains explicit. The generic paid output cap
is 32,768 tokens, clipped by model/context limits. Edit the table when a specific
model deserves a different cap; do not scatter special cases in the stream loop.

## How to replace a recommendation

Edit the readable table, keep a primary source and a concrete explanation of the
tradeoff, and run `python3 tools/build.py` to regenerate the single-file artifact.
Changing a recommendation does not mean silently rewriting existing user choices.
For a live investigation prefer native Pi settings and a disposable conversation
before promoting an opinion to everyone.

A review should check exact live ID and lifecycle; positive/selectable versus
mandatory reasoning; native API and opaque reasoning continuity; context/output
ceilings; direct tools versus additive Codemode; streaming usage and cache counts;
and whether a claimed improvement comes from vendor evaluation, a practitioner
report, or your own measured workload. Keep those evidence classes distinct.

There is deliberately no hard-coded current price table, temperature table,
cheapest-provider ranking, blanket maximum-effort default, or paid coding-ladder
requirement. Vendor benchmark settings are evidence, not necessarily good daily
interactive defaults. For example, GLM's vendor recommends maximum effort for
difficult coding; the shipped high preference is an interactive tradeoff, with
native `/thinking max` available deliberately.

