The actual 45,999-byte Sandcodex benchmark emitter payload is retained byte-for-byte in `benchmark_course_reference.py`. A thin runtime wrapper changes only bounded child-process cleanup. The Pi adapter, evidence parser and receipts are new. Existing Codex grades do
not become Pi grades: harness/tool/prompt/protocol differences matter.

| Rung | Task | Evidence sought |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | Batch boundary | Correct complete/partial batching and edge cases |
| 2 | Surgical label | Repair the assigned function without changing legacy behavior |
| 3 | Replay recovery | Preserve ordered replay, acknowledgement and re-publication semantics |
| 4 | Quota ladder | Choose eligible routes with account/route cooldowns and stable ordering |
| 5 | Atomic publication | Stage, verify, replace and clean up while preserving failures |

`smoke` runs rung 1; `patcher` runs 1–2; `maintainer`/`core` runs 1–3;
`ladder`/`operator` runs all five; `rung-N` selects one. Each rung carries ten
points: seven functional, one visible-test integrity, one assigned-file effect,
and one unrelated-file focus. The actual task registry/grader defines the checks.

A plain `sandpi openrouter` first uses a recent current-profile model with coding
floor at least two. Otherwise it tries at most two ungraded automatic candidates,
with a two-rung `patcher` run each. Default limits are 180 seconds and eight
provider starts per rung, including key failover attempts: at most four rungs and
32 provider starts in one cold-selection pass, often fewer. Infrastructure/quota failure stops the pass rather than burning through the catalog or assigning a low score. A typed missing-model result corroborated by the host broker cools only that physical model, consumes the candidate, and may advance to the next candidate inside the same two-model budget. No hidden full-catalog tournament runs at startup. If no pass is found, the guest
shell remains available and the error points to explicit choices/reports.

```bash
