are not implicitly leased through this coding bank. Ordinary text/multimodal-chat
capability still follows the native selected model.

`--no-extensions` disables optional discovery but sandpi explicitly keeps its
managed authentication adapter. The entire guest is yours; this convenience does
not prevent editing or replacing that adapter. A missing adapter cannot silently
use a stored real key: the on-disk Google/OpenRouter API-key value is a sentinel.

## Boundaries that have not changed

All Pi tools, extensions and benchmarks remain inside the VM by default. Host-root
mode is explicit. Actual leased API keys/access tokens are visible to the room;
Codex refresh tokens remain canonical host state. No promised spend firewall or
secret isolation exists against guest code reusing those leased credentials.

Sandpi instances share the new lease table. Unmodified Sandcodex does not join it:
same keys does not mean shared request counters or cooldown scheduler. Its known
stale-frontend OAuth reconciliation race remains a separately identified companion
fix. The exact supplied source is evaluated, never silently patched.

## Route health is not a coding grade

```bash
sandpi models
sandpi routes
sandpi routes --all --json
sandpi routes --reset --yes
```

OpenRouter free routes change independently of model capability. Sandpi therefore
keeps three orthogonal records:

| Record | Question | Automatic effect |
|---|---|---|
| Pi ladder grade | Can this exact profile repair code? | Establishes coding floor and possible Codemode promotion. |
| Hard cooldown | Is the key/group/model/route/provider currently leaseable? | Temporarily excludes the affected authority scope. |
| Passive route health | Has this exact catalog fingerprint recently completed requests? | Repeated failures rank it last; never excludes explicit use. |

A route starts `unknown`. One server, transport, timeout, unavailable-model, or
route-scoped throttle is merely observed. Repeated failure inside six hours marks
it degraded for a short bounded period. Success heals the passive record. After
seven days, or when capability/pricing metadata changes enough to change the
fingerprint, prior health becomes unknown. The ledger retains at most a bounded
number of recent rows and contains no credential values or conversation data.

`openrouter --model VENDOR/ID` and exact native resume still attempt the requested
model. `/sandmodels` keeps degraded routes in the list but puts them last. The
`best` resume fallback also prefers a nondegraded qualified model. GPT and Gemini
never use this signal and still bypass the coding ladder.

### Typed provider failures and key rotation

Only credential/account evidence should move to another credential before output:
invalid authentication, key-specific credit exhaustion, or an independently
annotated quota group. A typed missing model, provider overload/unavailability,
ambiguous platform throttle, timeout, context overflow, invalid request, refusal,
content-policy block, or permission failure does not prove another key is better.
Those outcomes stop the request, update the correct scope, and remain visible.

Any provider event exposed through Pi's raw-event hook, text/thinking delta, or tool call commits the attempt. A
later error is recorded but never replayed. OpenRouter may deliver a typed error
inside an HTTP-200 SSE stream, so HTTP status alone is not a completion receipt.

### Strict free-route request shape

For managed zero-price OpenRouter requests Sandpi keeps one exact model, forbids
plugin/model fallbacks, bounds every price field to zero, and sets
`require_parameters=true`. Pi's optional `stream_options` and `store` parameters
are suppressed because they can eliminate otherwise valid endpoints. The current
catalog's supported-parameter metadata chooses `max_tokens` versus
`max_completion_tokens`; absent unambiguous support, the conservative legacy field
is used. This is compatibility and cost routing, not a network or spend sandbox.

## Rev0010 exact-model recovery and request attempts

A hard model cooldown answers “should automatic work try this physical model
right now?” It is not a permanent ban on an explicitly requested route. Sandpi
therefore grants a narrow recovery marker only for an exact managed OpenRouter
`--model` and deterministic native resume. The lease broker may ignore the model
cooldown for that request, but it still enforces provider, quota-group, route and
key cooldowns and the exact zero-price/catalog contract.

Cooldown healing uses request start time, not completion order. Suppose request A
starts, request B later records a model failure, then A eventually succeeds. A
cannot clear B's newer failure. A recovery request C that starts after B and
succeeds can clear it. Equal timestamps and migrated legacy cooldowns without a
failure timestamp remain active until normal expiry or explicit reset.

One opaque request scope is created before any key lookup. Attempts 1..8 for the
same logical provider call retain that scope while credential fingerprints change.
The scheduler stores fixed routing facts only and keeps at most 2,048 events for
30 days:

```bash
sandpi keys openrouter --events 20
sandpi keys gemini --events 20 --json
```

Inside Pi, `/keys recent` (alias `/keys history`) shows the newest attempts.
`attempt-start` and `attempt-finish` identify model, public key ID, quota group,
timing, outcome, HTTP status when available, commit state, exact-model probe use,
and healed/retained cooldown scopes. They never contain prompts, answers, tool
content, provider payloads, raw errors or credential values.

The journal is observational. It cannot authorize model fallback, infer coding
quality, replay a committed request, or establish that the model completed the
user's task. Coding grade, hard cooldown, passive route health and request history
remain separate records.

