machine-readable table is `src/pi/model-defaults.json`.

These are Sandpi opinions, not measured coding grades. Their source URLs,
rationale, supported Pi version, canonical revision and review window live beside
the code in `src/pi/model-defaults.json`. Live catalog capabilities still win. If
an alias points at a new canonical revision, Sandpi withdraws that preset rather
than carrying old defaults forward or guessing a successor. The exact model
remains usable through `--model`. GPT, Gemini and paid models do not run the
free-model coding ladder.

Unknown priced models remain usable. They start with Pi's direct tools, native
provider behavior and a generic context-sized compaction profile. An advertised
text-only model is usable for conversation without fictional coding tools.

## Permission and selection are separate

`--paid` permits priced OpenRouter **chat** in this Pi process. It does not
persist in the project machine, Pi session, room selector or user configuration.
It does not switch your current model. Plain `sandpi openrouter` remains a
free-only entry with the existing bounded coding qualification.

Inside a process with a priced catalog already loaded:

```text
/sandpaid
/sandpaid on
/sandpaid off
/sandmodels paid
/sanddefaults
/sandcost
/sandroute
```

`/sandpaid` does not submit a prompt, choose a model or retry a failed request.
`/reload` preserves the current process choice; a new process starts with paid
access off unless explicitly enabled. A resumed priced session with paid access
off remains that session and model, but inference waits for explicit opt-in.
To populate the priced catalog for the first time, enter through `sandpi --paid`.
Later plain room entry can reuse that cached catalog without granting paid access.

From the guest shell:

```bash
pi --sandpi-paid
pi --sandpi-paid -c
pi --sandpi-paid --sandpi-max-price 5,25 --model openrouter/VENDOR/EXACT_ID
```

The guest wrapper consumes the `--sandpi-*` flags and starts upstream Pi with a
fresh invocation document. Optional packages, native tools, MCP and project
resources continue to use Pi's normal configuration. Raw flags after `--` remain
prompt data; text mentioning a paid flag cannot enable it.

## Useful defaults without taking Pi over

Codemode is additive: capable reviewed models can orchestrate tool calls and
reduce bulky results in JavaScript, but direct tools stay available. Sandpi does
not require every tool action to be a Codemode program. Existing native tool
choices, disabled tools, dynamic MCP tools, `--tools`, and passthrough still win.
Use `/sandprofile classic` or `/sandprofile passthrough` when a task or package
works better without this recommendation.

Reviewed paid definitions permit up to 32,768 output tokens, further limited by
the actual model and context. This is a ceiling, not a requested answer length.
Reasoning may consume part of a model's output allowance. Compaction reserves
more room than the small free-model defaults, without claiming the entire
advertised context is always practical to fill. Explicit user compaction and
thinking settings are preserved by the existing reversible overlay mechanism.

Thinking recommendations apply to startup defaults. Pi still owns interactive
thinking changes and branch history. Sandpi does not repeatedly force `high`
after you choose something else. Models that always reason but do not expose an
effort selector keep provider-managed reasoning. Temperature and top-p are not
blanket-overridden by model family. Sampling and deeper task-specific tuning
remain Pi/provider configuration.

## Costs and endpoint selection

```bash
sandpi models --paid --refresh
sandpi --paid --preset kimi --max-price 5,25
```

The optional cap means **$5 per million input tokens and $25 per million output
tokens**, supplied as gateway endpoint-selection constraints. It is not $30
maximum spend, a hard session budget, or a total-cost estimate. No cap is invented
when you omit it. Native stricter constraints are retained where combined.

The model list, `/sanddefaults`, and `/sandroute` show base input/output price
estimates from the current catalog. Pi receives nonzero rates and streaming
usage, so its normal session accounting is no longer incorrectly zero for paid
routes. Missing cache rates use the input rate as an estimate rather than free.

**These are estimates, not invoices.** Long-context tiers, time-dependent price
overrides, endpoint differences, cache writes, cache discounts and account fees
can change billed cost. OpenRouter account controls and billing records are the
authority for spend. An agent-owned guest is not a billing security boundary.

Sandpi does not force cheapest-first provider order: a cheap endpoint can be the
wrong tradeoff for tool reliability or cache reuse. The physical model is fixed,
but OpenRouter can still use its native provider selection among endpoints for
that model. Different-model fallback, paid server plugins, and separate auxiliary
image/classifier/deferred operations are not silently enabled by paid chat access.
Those are separate future integrations, not free side effects of model selection.

## See what a request actually reported

`/sandcost` reports upstream OpenRouter charges **separately** from Pi's native
estimate. Cache-read counts show whether a particular run actually reused input,
not merely whether a session-affinity hint was sent. The report distinguishes
active branch versus whole session file, accounted versus missing attempts,
interrupted responses, and BYOK upstream costs. It does not poll the billing API,
turn estimates into reported values, or send bookkeeping into model context.

The report observes only managed OpenRouter streams after this revision. A
partial stream can lack final usage even when the provider charged for work.
Native Messages transports may report tokens without a final charge. In both
cases, cost stays unknown. Compaction preserves original entries; forks/clones
inherit past expenses, not new requests. Overlapping files are not additive.
Read `OPENROUTER-USAGE.md` before treating these subtotals as billing evidence.

## Several keys, without throwing away every cache

The canonical Sandcodex OpenRouter bank is still the only bank. Mark keys that
share an actual account/quota domain with the same group:

```bash
sandpi keys openrouter
sandpi keys openrouter --key work-a --group account-a
sandpi keys openrouter --key work-b --group account-a
sandpi keys openrouter --key personal --group account-b
```

For paid chat, a hash of native session identity and exact model selects a soft
account-group preference. Before that rendezvous rank, fresh account telemetry
prefers known paid credentials over unknown credentials, and unknown credentials
over accounts observed as free-only or rejected with 401/403. Every class remains
fallback-eligible so a stale/racing observation cannot create a local outage.
New conversations distribute across equally eligible groups. Inside
the preferred eligible group, least-in-flight and least-recently-used rotation
still balances keys. Explicit pins, rejection, cooldown and exclusions take
precedence. Unannotated keys behave as separate groups; Sandpi cannot infer that
two secret strings belong to one account.

Stable `session_id` and `x-session-id` values accompany paid requests without
changing messages or signed reasoning. They help OpenRouter maintain conversation
routing across normal turns and compaction. They do not guarantee a cache hit,
shared quotas, or additional allowance. Free daily counters do not suppress paid
routes just because an account's free allowance is exhausted.

Provider failures retain their scoped handling and bounded attempts. An
ineligible credential can change before output, not the selected model. After
observed content/tool activity the request is never transparently replayed.
Normal prompt-cache use and native explicit retention choices remain available;
paid routes do not inherit the free-only long-TTL suppression. Background cache-warming requests
remain disabled on managed banks to avoid extra hidden inference.

