## Move one active room between hosts

Portable backups capture the private NixOS/Pi machine and leave host-global
authority behind.

```bash
sandpi down
sandpi backup create /mnt/archive/app-main --store manifest
sandpi backup inspect /mnt/archive/app-main --verify

# On the target host, from the anchored project root:
sandpi backup import /mnt/archive/app-main migrated --activate
sandpi
```

Use `--store full` when the target should not depend on its configured
substituters for the room's retained lower-store closure:

```bash
sandpi backup create /mnt/archive/app-main-full --store full
sandpi backup import /mnt/archive/app-main-full migrated --activate
```

A bundle is a directory, not an opaque tarball. Copy it with a tool that preserves
large sparse files, then run `backup inspect --verify` after transport. The
destination path and target room must not already exist.

Backup v2 records the exact source runtime contract. An imported room starts
normally when that contract matches the target launcher; otherwise the first boot
uses the cold runtime-upgrade transaction described below. Legacy v1 bundles are
accepted but deliberately import with unknown runtime compatibility.

### Resolve runtime rollback before backup

A Lifeboat slot and a portable backup answer different questions. The slot keeps
both sides of one local runtime transition. The backup carries exactly one active
machine. Sandpi therefore refuses backup while `sandpi upgrade` reports any live
slot:

```bash
sandpi upgrade
sandpi upgrade accept --yes       # keep the active side and discard rollback
# or
sandpi upgrade rollback --yes     # return to the source side
sandpi upgrade forward --yes      # restore a retained ready target side
sandpi upgrade retry --yes        # restore source and clear a failed attempt
```

After the slot is resolved, create the backup again. The bundle never contains
`active`, `forward.raw`, source metadata preimages, or other upgrade-journal files.

### Backup scope

Included:

- one stopped room's active sparse `root.raw`;
- native Pi sessions and all other guest-local state as opaque disk bytes;
- exact room-local lower Nix store paths;
- source identity, runtime contract, disk digest, exclusions, and optional
  machine profile; and
- an optional local Nix binary cache for `--store full`.

Excluded:

- the live host project mounted at `/workspace`;
- canonical Sandcodex GPT, Gemini, and OpenRouter credentials;
- logs, checkpoints, host session observations, active entries, VMM/process state;
- port forwards; and
- runtime rollback/forward evidence.

Back up the host project and credential material through their own normal
procedures. `--keep-machine-profile` may copy the source CPU/RAM/disk/cache
envelope, but TCP and UDP forwards are always cleared on import.

### Checkpoint source

A named stopped-room checkpoint can be exported without restoring it first:

```bash
sandpi backup create DEST --checkpoint before-upgrade --store full
```

The checkpoint disk and checkpoint-local runtime/machine metadata are the source.
The room's checkpoint collection itself is not copied.



## Enter one room from any subdirectory

Sandpi now separates the persistent project root from Pi's native working
directory. The nearest existing `.sandpi` ancestor selects the room and the whole
live workspace share; the directory where the command was invoked becomes Pi's
working directory inside that room. No Git-root heuristic is used.

```bash
cd /work/app
sandpi project --init          # optional but useful before first subdirectory entry
sandpi                         # room root /work/app; Pi cwd /workspace

cd /work/app/src/parser
sandpi                         # same room; Pi cwd /workspace/src/parser
sandpi --RESUME                # latest native Pi session for this exact cwd
sandpi project                 # prints project root plus host/guest cwd
```

On first use, when no ancestor contains `.sandpi`, the exact current directory is
still the project root. Initialize or launch once from the repository root when
one room should cover its descendants. `--project /work/app` selects that root
explicitly and preserves the current relative cwd only when the caller is inside
it; otherwise the guest starts at `/workspace`.

The selected workdir must resolve to an existing directory inside the donated
project. Sandpi refuses `/`, broad system roots, the whole home directory,
`.sandpi` itself, Sandpi state, canonical Sandcodex credential roots, or a project
that contains those control roots. In VM mode `.sandpi` remains masked even though
the project root is shared live.

Pi itself groups sessions, discovers `.pi` resources and context files, and
resolves project settings by cwd. Consequently `--RESUME` is exact-cwd and
execution-mode scoped: an observation from `/workspace/other` or from bare-metal
can never supply the profile hint for `/workspace/src`. The host observation is
content-free and records only mode/cwd alongside the existing native identity and
model/profile lifecycle fields; Pi remains the sole parser of session JSONL.

## Identify or recover the project and room before changing it

A room is keyed by the project's stable host-only anchor plus `--name`. Before the
first real launch, the anchor is a side-effect-free preview of the canonical path;
the launch writes `PROJECT/.sandpi/project.json` without changing the legacy room
ID. The `.sandpi` directory is masked from `/workspace` in VM mode.

```bash
sandpi project
sandpi project --json
sandpi status --all
sandpi inspect --project /work/app --name experiment
sandpi inspect --project /work/app --name experiment --live
```

### Missing anchor

An ignored-file cleanup, archive restore, or `git clean -d -x` can remove
`.sandpi`. Sandpi does not immediately invent another room. It scans bounded host
metadata and classifies candidates:

| Match | Meaning | Normal launch |
|---|---|---|
| `exact-object` | central path and device/inode both match | automatic preview/recovery |
| `same-object-moved` | device/inode match after rename | automatic preview/rebind |
| `path-replaced` | same pathname, different object | explicit decision required |
| `room-path` | strict room receipt names this path, binding absent | explicit recovery required |

`plan` never recreates the file. A unique strong binding supplies the effective
namespace and room ID only in memory. The next real launch holds the project lock,
revalidates the candidate, writes the anchor, migrates stopped host metadata if
needed, and publishes the binding. Any failure removes only the exact anchor that
transaction created.

```bash
sandpi plan
sandpi project --json
sandpi project --recover --yes
```

If several namespaces match, unqualified recovery refuses to guess. Copy the
printed namespace SHA-256 prefix, not the raw namespace:

```bash
sandpi project --recover 4f92a07c --yes
```

The prefix must contain 8–64 hexadecimal characters and identify one namespace.
A failed or ambiguous selection changes nothing. Recovery scans at most 4,096
binding entries and 4,096 room entries, and never reads native Pi session JSONL,
prompts, model responses, tool output, guest packages, or project contents.

### Rename, copy, and replacement

After a same-filesystem rename, run ordinary commands from the new path. Device and
inode prove that it is the same directory, so even a live room can be entered; the
next stopped launch transactionally updates room metadata and transcript-free host
session observations. Pi's native session bytes stay on the guest disk and are not
rewritten.

A copied `.sandpi` anchor is refused while the original path still exists. Choose
one explicit outcome:

```bash
sandpi project --new --yes       # this copy gets independent future rooms
sandpi project --rebind --yes    # existing anchor intentionally takes over here
```

An unchanged pathname is not enough. If the current directory has a different
`(st_dev, st_ino)` from the central binding, Sandpi reports
`replaced-at-same-path`. This covers restored archives, replacement checkouts, and
cross-filesystem copy/delete moves. With an existing anchor, use `--rebind --yes`
to keep the machine or `--new --yes` to split the lineage. With a missing anchor,
inspect candidates and use `--recover [SHA256_PREFIX] --yes` or `--new --yes`.

`--rebind` requires every matching room and host entry to be stopped. `--new`
refuses to orphan rooms already owned by the current directory object. A running
virtiofs share continues to refer to the object mounted at boot, so replacement
inode entry is refused until stop/relaunch.

The central binding is under `SANDPI_HOME/projects/` and records only namespace,
path, device/inode, timestamps, and revision. Anchor/binding files are bounded,
single-link, user-owned regular files. Binding filenames must match the namespace
digest. The project `.sandpi` directory and host recovery roots cannot be symlinks
or writable by group/other users.

Live inspection queries the recorded user service and host-only CH API with
bounded deadlines. It reports process liveness, room limits, service outcome,
VMM state when available, sparse disk capacity/allocation, host free space,
cache path count and incomplete restore receipts. A stopped service may already
have been collected; missing live systemd data is not evidence of a new failure.

## Inspect and control a Sandpi runtime upgrade

A room records the exact Sandpi/Pi/Nix/Cloud Hypervisor contract that most
recently reached guest command readiness. Upgrading the standalone launcher does
not immediately rewrite that evidence. Inspection is read-only:

```bash
sandpi plan
sandpi upgrade
sandpi upgrade --json
sandpi inspect
sandpi storage
```

For an existing room whose stored contract differs from the current executable,
the first stopped launch creates one cold rollback slot before Cloud Hypervisor
starts. The slot contains a sparse/reflink copy of `root.raw`, the exact bytes of
`room.json`, and a fixed manifest. A new room has no prior machine to protect and
publishes its first contract only after its room-ID SSH probe succeeds.

A successful boot leaves the rollback in `ready` state. Keeping it is valid,
but remember that it is a whole-guest-disk generation: guest-local Pi sessions,
packages, MCP state and guest Nix files created afterward belong to the newer disk.

```bash
sandpi                         # boot current runtime; retain old cold disk
sandpi upgrade                 # stage=ready
sandpi upgrade accept --yes    # keep current newer disk; remove old rollback
```

A later different Sandpi runtime automatically accepts an older *ready* slot
before creating one new rollback. Sandpi never stacks an unbounded chain of room
disks.

If the guest does not reach command readiness, another launch is blocked. Choose
one of the explicit recovery paths:

```bash
sandpi upgrade rollback --yes  # restore old whole disk + exact old room metadata
sandpi upgrade retry --yes     # restore old state, discard failed/new disk, try fresh
```

`rollback` retains the failed/new disk as `forward.raw` for inspection and can
resume after interruption from durable disk names. When the target had already
reached readiness, the rollback is reversible:

```bash
sandpi upgrade forward --yes   # restore newer disk + exact newer room metadata
sandpi upgrade accept --yes    # accept whichever disk is current; remove opposite disk
```

`forward` is refused for a target that never reached guest readiness. `retry`
first ensures the old source state is current, then removes the active slot and
its retained forward disk; the next launch prepares a fresh rollback. Accepting a
completed rollback likewise keeps the old source disk and deliberately discards
the newer guest state. All destructive choices require `--yes`.

Rollback rewinds guest-local Pi sessions, installed packages, MCP configuration,
guest Nix state and every other file on the room disk. `forward` restores those
opaque bytes from the retained newer disk. Neither direction touches the live
host project, canonical credentials, host Nix store, host observations or Cloud
Hypervisor RAM/device state, and Sandpi never parses Pi session JSONL.

Until the slot is accepted or cleared, Sandpi refuses structural changes that
would make its exact preimage ambiguous:

```text
disk-grow
restore CHECKPOINT
machine --reset --yes
project --rebind --yes (or automatic moved-project rebind)
rename TARGET
```

A ready room may be cloned; the clone receives the current guest disk and current
runtime contract but not the source rollback slot. Creating an ordinary
checkpoint is also allowed because it does not mutate the source disk.


## Inspect or change the persistent machine profile

A stable project anchor plus `--name` identifies one persistent machine. After
the first successful launch, Sandpi saves only the VM-level choices needed to
recreate that machine: vCPUs, total room memory budget, sparse disk capacity, Nix
store-cache mode, and TCP/UDP passt forwards.

```bash
sandpi machine
sandpi machine --json
```

`machine` reports either the saved profile or a current-host preview when the room
has not launched since this feature was introduced. It also evaluates whether the
saved CPU and memory envelope still fits the current host's half-resource policy.
A profile that was valid on a larger host fails visibly rather than being silently
shrunk.

Plain `sandpi` and `sandpi up` reuse the profile. To change one field, stop the
room and launch once with the explicit replacement:

```bash
sandpi down
sandpi --cpus 4 --memory 8G
sandpi --store-cache base  # accepted compatibility alias; promoted to host
sandpi --publish 8080:80 --publish 3000:3000
sandpi --publish-udp 5353:5353
```

Repeated `--publish` values are the complete new TCP set, not additions to an
invisible old list. The UDP option behaves the same way. Clear a saved set with:

```bash
sandpi --no-publish
sandpi --no-publish-udp
```

An already-running room accepts an explicitly repeated value only if it equals the
live configuration. Actual CPU, memory, disk, cache, or port changes require a
stopped room because the VMM, backends, passt listeners, and host cgroup already
exist.

To forget every saved machine default while preserving the durable machine:

```bash
sandpi machine --reset --yes
```

Reset does not remove `root.raw`, Pi sessions, guest packages, checkpoints, host
project files, shared credentials, model grades, route observations, or key-bank
state. The next launch uses current-host defaults plus any flags supplied then.
The actual regular `root.raw` size remains authoritative, and `disk-grow` or
checkpoint restore synchronizes the profile's capacity.

Provider, model, native session, thinking, tool, key-pin, prompt, and credential
state never enters this profile. Those remain Pi/process choices. `--BARE-METAL`
is independent of VM metadata and rejects VM-only machine flags rather than
pretending to apply them to host root.

## Branch a stopped machine without copying the workspace

Named rooms are separate guest machines attached to one anchored host project.
Use `rooms` to inspect them and `clone` to branch the guest state of a stopped
source room:

```bash
sandpi rooms
sandpi rooms --json
sandpi clone experiment
sandpi clone experiment --checkpoint before-upgrade
sandpi --name experiment
```

The source is the explicit `--name` override when present, otherwise the project's saved active room (initially the implicit `default`); the positional argument is the new target name. Both current-disk and checkpoint-origin cloning
require the source VMM and every host entry to be stopped. A shutdown drain also
blocks cloning. The target name must not already exist as a file, directory, or
symlink.

The clone copies `root.raw`, which includes native Pi sessions, guest-local Pi
packages and writable Nix state. It does **not** copy the host workspace: source
and target both see the same live project at `/workspace`, and Pi starts in the
same relative cwd chosen by the invoking command. It also excludes source
checkpoints, logs, status/process records, entry leases and transcript-free host
session observations.

CPU, memory, disk and Nix-cache settings are copied when the source has a saved
machine profile. TCP and UDP forwards are always cleared because the two rooms may
later run concurrently. Add new forwards explicitly on the target's first launch.

The disk copier prefers reflink/CoW, then copies sparse data extents, then uses a
zero-scanning sparse fallback. Logical capacity is preserved, but physical space
may grow later as source and target diverge. A clone still depends on Sandpi's
retained host Nix-store lease and is not a portable backup.

Publication is transactional. Sandpi builds a complete target below
`SANDPI_HOME/trash`, fsyncs it, then uses Linux atomic no-replace rename into
`rooms/`. A failed or interrupted pre-publication operation cannot overwrite an
existing room. Operation-owned staging is removed when possible; `clean --trash
--yes` remains the explicit recovery path for abandoned quarantine state.

`room.json` records a bounded `sandpi.room-lineage.v1` entry. It is one-hop machine
provenance, not a transcript and not proof that project files match the source at
clone time. Pi remains the only parser of conversations inside each disk.

## Choose which named machine plain entry uses

Named rooms are machine branches. Pi sessions remain native state inside each
branch, while the project workspace stays the same live host directory. The
host-only project selector chooses only the room that an omitted `--name` means:

```bash
sandpi rooms
sandpi use experiment
sandpi                         # experiment
sandpi --name default          # default once; saved choice remains experiment
sandpi use --reset             # implicit default again
```

`rooms` marks the saved active room with `*`. When `--name` selects another room
for one command, `>` marks that override; `#` means both names coincide. The
selector is not copied into the VM and Pi cannot alter it.

A saved choice must point to an existing room. If it does not, ordinary room
commands fail with a repair instruction instead of creating a fresh disk under
that name. `sandpi rooms` and `sandpi use` remain available:

```bash
sandpi rooms
sandpi use another-existing-room
sandpi use --reset
```

`clone TARGET --activate` commits the target first and then selects it. Deleting
the active room resets selection before removal and restores the exact prior
selector if removal fails. `project --new --yes` clears a copied selector because
the new project namespace must not inherit the source project's active machine.

Bare-metal is intentionally separate: `sandpi gpt --BARE-METAL` uses the default
host-root grouping even when the VM project prefers another room. Add
`--name NAME` only when that grouping is deliberate.

## Rename a stopped machine without copying its disk

Use rename when the machine is right but its project-scoped name is not:

```bash
sandpi rooms
sandpi rename feature-v2
sandpi rename archived-experiment --name experiment
```

The selected room is the source unless `--name SOURCE` explicitly chooses a
room for this command. The positional argument is always the target. Rename is
strictly offline: the VMM, every owner/secondary host entry, any shutdown drain,
and both source/target resource reservations must be absent. An existing target
file, directory, or symlink is never replaced.

Sandpi moves the complete room directory with Linux
`renameat2(RENAME_NOREPLACE)`. `root.raw` therefore keeps the same inode: native
Pi sessions, guest packages, writable Nix state and logs are not copied or
rewritten. The live host workspace is not moved because every project room still
mounts the same anchored directory at `/workspace`.

A bounded transaction file moves with the room and advances through host identity
migration. Re-run the exact same command after interruption. The retry may start
before or after directory publication, checkpoint migration, transcript-free VM
observation rekey, or active-selector publication. A final `rename.json` receipt
makes the post-commit/pre-output window idempotent too.

The migrated state is deliberately narrow:

- `room.json`, bounded rename history and stopped launch/status identity;
- checkpoint manifest room IDs, without touching checkpoint disk bytes;
- transcript-free **VM** session observations;
- the saved active-room selector when it still names the source.

Native Pi session JSONL, prompts, responses, tools, credentials, coding grades,
key-bank state, workspace files and historical clone lineage are untouched.
Bare-metal state and observations remain under the original room ID because a VM
rename must not silently relocate explicit host-root work.

If the active room is renamed, plain `sandpi` follows it. Renaming to `default`
returns to the implicit default selector. A newer deliberate `sandpi use` choice
made after an interrupted rename is preserved rather than overwritten by the
older transaction.

## See who is using a room before stopping it

A detached room may have several host terminals and automation processes inside
it. Sandpi tracks each secondary Pi/shell/exec entry with a content-free host
lease bound to the launcher's PID and Linux start ticks.

```bash
sandpi entries
sandpi entries --all
sandpi entries --all --json
```

The record includes only entry ID, owner/secondary role, Pi interface, terminal
flag, host PID/start identity, start time, and room lifecycle. It never includes
prompts, Pi arguments, transcripts, model responses, tool output, credentials,
environment, or guest file paths. The owner-terminal row is synthesized from the
room launch record; it is not another writable lease.

Safe shutdown is cooperative:

```bash
sandpi down               # refuse any live owner/secondary entry
sandpi down --wait 30     # close admission and wait up to 30 seconds
sandpi down --force       # explicit termination despite remaining entries
sandpi down --json        # structured stop receipt
```

A drain gate is installed before occupancy is read. New `sandpi`, `attach`, and
`exec` entries fail while that gate is live, so the active set can only shrink.
The gate is tied to the drain process's PID/start identity and self-clears after a
crash. Dead entry leases self-prune. When available, the final supervisor signal
uses a pidfd after rechecking `/proc` start ticks, avoiding accidental signalling
of a recycled PID.

`--force` is intentionally not implied by a timeout. If `--wait` expires, shutdown
still refuses unless `--force` was also supplied. A successful stop opens the drain
gate and prunes dead leases. A host frontend that is still unwinding retains its
PID/start-pinned lease until that process exits, so persistent cleanup cannot race
it. The room disk, native Pi sessions, guest packages, host workspace, and canonical
credentials remain.

## Enter Pi more than once without carrying stale intent

The first Pi process, later guest-shell Pi processes and concurrent attached
shells share the persistent room but not launch intent. The wrapper publishes a
unique invocation record for each process under the room's Pi agent runtime area.
The room-global base record is never reused as an executable process policy.

```bash
sandpi gemini      # first process only
# exit Pi
pi                 # new process, native defaults/new session
pi -c              # new process, fresh continue preflight
pi -r              # new process, native picker
```

The small package/credential/settings composition step is protected by a bounded
lock so simultaneous launches cannot corrupt Pi settings. The lock is released
before Pi runs; it does not serialize conversations, provider requests or session
files. Old invocation records are pruned to a bounded set.

If shared credential management is later removed for one provider, the next
wrapped invocation restores any displaced Pi-native credential. Dynamic model
defaults are similarly removed only when still equal to Sandpi's own prior value.
User-edited values survive.

## Inventory and retire persistent state

A room is a machine, not a disposable process. Inspect its logical and allocated
storage before deleting anything:

```bash
sandpi storage
sandpi storage --all
sandpi storage --all --json
```

The disk capacity is the sparse file's logical size. Allocated bytes are host
filesystem blocks and can be much smaller. The inventory also shows the saved
machine profile so unexpected CPU/RAM/cache/forward behavior can be explained
without reading Pi state. Reflink checkpoint block counts are not necessarily
unique physical usage. Shared `/nix/store` data is represented by the host lease's
path count instead of being charged to each room.

Plain cleanup is intentionally safe:

```bash
sandpi clean
```

It requires a stopped room and deletes only superseded launcher-log directories.
The newest run and the run referenced by `status.json` remain. The current disk,
checkpoints, native Pi sessions, guest packages, project directory and canonical
credentials remain.

Durable cleanup is always explicit:

```bash
sandpi clean --logs --yes
sandpi clean --checkpoints --yes
sandpi clean --room --yes
sandpi clean --trash --yes
sandpi clean --cache --yes
```

`--room` removes the selected guest disk and all state inside that guest, including
Pi sessions and user-installed guest packages. It does not remove the live host
workspace or `~/.sandstuff/sandcodex`. It also removes only the room's transcript-
free host observation rows and a stale room reservation.

Before recursive removal, Sandpi acquires the global lifecycle lock and the room's
preparation/lifetime locks, verifies the room is stopped, refuses any live owner or
secondary host entry and any active shutdown drain, validates ownership and
single-link disk files, rejects unexpected symlinks, and reads `/proc/self/mountinfo`
to refuse any mount at or below the target. The validated room is atomically renamed
under `~/.sandstuff/sandpi/trash` before physical deletion. If metadata update fails
before bytes are deleted, the room is renamed back. If physical deletion is
interrupted, inspect `sandpi storage --all`, then use `clean --trash --yes` after
confirming the quarantined target is no longer needed.

The host Nix-store lease is cumulative because persistent upper layers can reference
lower-store paths. `clean --cache --yes` therefore refuses while any `root.raw`
exists in a room, checkpoint or quarantine. It removes Sandpi GC-root symlinks,
lease metadata and registration snapshots only. It does not run
`nix-collect-garbage`, remove Nix store objects directly, or delete build out-links.

## Take an experiment checkpoint

Finish/stop the selected room, then:

```bash
sandpi checkpoint before-experiment
sandpi checkpoint
sandpi
```

Checkpoints are immutable to ordinary checkpoint/restore commands: a duplicate
label is rejected. They are under `rooms/<room-id>/checkpoints/<label>/`, with a
`root.raw` and `checkpoint.json`. They preserve guest files, private Nix additions
and Pi sessions at that disk point. They do not snapshot running memory or include
workspace files, canonical credentials, host cache contents, or host services.

An abrupt VM stop may leave ext4 needing normal journal recovery. A disk copy
after such a stop is crash-consistent, not proof that every application flushed
its own state. Prefer normal guest exit for clean checkpoints.

## Restore without losing the current disk

```bash
sandpi restore before-experiment --yes
sandpi checkpoint
sandpi inspect
sandpi
```

The room must be stopped. Restore copies the desired checkpoint to a replacement
file, retains the current disk as `before-restore-*`, and swaps root.raw atomically.
It records the safety label in its JSON output and `last-restore.json`. Restoring
the safety label performs the same operation and preserves another rollback point.
The selected checkpoint's logical capacity becomes the saved machine-profile disk
capacity as well; the replaced regular `root.raw` remains authoritative if a
metadata write is interrupted.

**Your live host project stays at its current state.** A restored earlier Pi
conversation can therefore see later source files. Use a matching Git commit or
your usual project snapshot only when you deliberately want that relationship.
Restoring a guest also never rolls back the current shared provider credentials.

Reflinks share physical extents on supporting filesystems; the fallback copies
allocated extents and preserves holes. Fully allocated images need correspondingly
more free space. Allocation shown by `stat` may count reflink-shared blocks more
than once; it is not unique physical consumption. There is no automatic checkpoint
retention/pruning policy. Keep enough space for the safety copy and replacement.

Checkpoints require the same retained host Nix lower-store lease. They are not
standalone backups suitable for copying to a new host or deleting global cache
roots. Export portable project data separately.

## Recover an interrupted operation

`inspect` flags incomplete restore metadata and a capacity mismatch. Disk capacity
on the next launch comes from the actual root.raw, not stale room.json. Preserve
the checkpoint directory, current root.raw and last-restore.json before diagnosing.
Do not manually delete automatic safety checkpoints to make a warning disappear.

A failed copy before replacement leaves the current disk intact. A failure after
the atomic replacement may mean the new disk is already selected while the final
receipt is incomplete; use the retained safety label to choose the desired state.

## Grow storage

```bash
sandpi disk-grow 150G
```

This is an offline expansion only; shrinking/reformatting an existing disk is
rejected. The filesystem expands on the next NixOS boot, and the saved machine
profile is updated to the new capacity. A checkpoint made before growth retains
its original disk capacity; restoring it restores both the actual capacity and the
profile value. The default 100G disk is a sparse logical ceiling, not preallocated
host space.
Host cache roots, Nix build outputs, logs and checkpoints consume space outside it.

## Protocol-friendly invocation

```bash
git diff | sandpi gpt -- --print 'Review this diff'
sandpi gemini -- --mode json 'Explain the project' > events.jsonl
sandpi openrouter --model VENDOR/MODEL -- --mode rpc --no-session
```

`--pi-only` is implied for these headless routes. For RPC, open pipes, begin
reading stdout, send LF-terminated JSON objects, correlate responses, and close
stdin after the desired result. `get_state` is suitable for a transport probe
without asking a model to work. `tests/headless-smoke.py` provides a complete
bounded example, not an autonomous job scheduler.

## Support report

```bash
sandpi report ./room-report.tar.gz
sandpi report ./room-report-with-logs.tar.gz --include-logs
```

The default report is metadata only. It excludes native sessions, workspace
files, disk contents, key files, environment, Pi argv and command text. Log tails
are opt-in, bounded and best-effort redacted. Guest-emitted proprietary text may
still be present in logs; review those bundles before sharing. Existing report
filenames are not overwritten. Generating a report neither restarts nor repairs
a room automatically.

## Cache decisions

`--store-cache host` is the effective policy. The legacy `--store-cache base`
spelling remains accepted for saved profiles and scripts, but is reported and
executed as a promotion to `host`. A smaller registration set is unsafe with the
single full-host-store lower view: guest GC could otherwise attempt to remove
visible, unregistered paths. Keep host GC roots until all dependent room disks and
checkpoints have been intentionally retired.

Host objects installed after a room starts may appear in its shared lower store
without joining the durable lease. Do not combine host-side Nix mutation with
guest GC. Restarting preserves the first snapshot and adds only a newly required
Sandpi runtime closure; it does not absorb unrelated host builds. The guest can
independently install what it needs into its private upper layer. No store bytes
are copied merely to assemble a view.

An unchanged stopped-room launch should report `fast_path: true` in the private
