# Exact-ID catalog-delta focus — rev0081 / Waypoint

Waypoint adds an invocation-local exact-ID query to the one retained OpenRouter
catalog delta.

```bash
sandpi models --diff previous current --focus vendor/model
sandpi models --diff previous current \
  --focus vendor/model-a \
  --focus vendor/model-b
sandpi models --paid --diff previous current --focus vendor/paid-model --json
sandpi models --refresh --diff previous current --focus vendor/model
```

`--focus` is valid only with `--diff`. It can be repeated for at most 32 unique,
case-sensitive normalized model IDs. Duplicates are removed while preserving the
caller's order.

The query is not saved. Sandpi does not poll the catalog, notify later, refresh
unless `--refresh` was explicitly supplied. It does not select a model. This is
not a persistent watchlist.

## Why this is more than shell filtering

The unfiltered JSON delta already supports ordinary shell filtering for added,
removed and metadata-changed rows. It intentionally omits unchanged model rows.
Filtering that output therefore cannot distinguish:

- an exact ID present and unchanged in both snapshots;
- an exact ID absent from both snapshots; and
- an exact ID whose change is outside a caller's text-filter mistake.

Waypoint resolves those states against the two already validated local snapshots.
It adds no catalog archive and no second source of model truth.

## Five explicit states

Every requested ID receives one state in the requested diff direction:

| State | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `added` | Absent from the earlier snapshot and present in the later one. |
| `removed` | Present in the earlier snapshot and absent from the later one. |
| `changed` | Present in both, with at least one compared normalized field changed. |
| `unchanged` | Present in both, with no change among the compared normalized fields. |
| `absent` | Absent from both snapshots. |

The direction still matters. `current previous` reverses added and removed just as
the ordinary delta does. `removed` remains local snapshot evidence, not proof of
provider retirement or endpoint unavailability.

## Human and structured output

Human output keeps the whole-pair counts, then shows only the requested IDs. It
omits unrelated added/removed/changed rows so a small query stays small. Whole-
snapshot normalization-counter changes remain disclosed but are not attributed to
focused IDs.

`--json` retains the complete ordinary delta and adds a `focus` array. Each focused
row contains:

- exact ID, state and changed-field names;
- bounded before/after identity, capability, transport and lifecycle evidence;
- at most 64 normalized supported-parameter names plus an omitted count;
- a recognized pricing projection: base known rate dimensions, Pi's configured
  per-million estimate, and counts of prompt-tier, scheduled and combined/other
  overrides; and
- bounded fingerprints from the normalized snapshots.

The pricing projection does not copy arbitrary unknown provider keys. It is still
catalog metadata, not an invoice, endpoint price limit or prediction of the next
request.

## Scope and ownership

Focus operates on the exact normalized ID. It does not use `canonical_slug` to
merge IDs, resolve aliases through OpenRouter's single-model endpoint, infer a
rename, or select a successor.

Free and full histories remain separate. Without `--paid`, the query addresses the
free predecessor/current pair. With `--paid`, it addresses the full pair.

Inspection is local unless `--refresh` is present. It makes no credential, credit,
account, Pi or inference request. It does not alter the catalog loaded by a running
Pi process, paid authorization, native model settings, thinking, tools, routing or
compaction.

## Validation scope

Offline contracts cover:

- all five exact-ID states;
- forward and reverse direction;
- duplicate, invalid and excessive focus lists;
- bounded supported-parameter and pricing projections;
- whole-delta counts and omission evidence alongside narrow human output;
