# OpenRouter catalog recency in `/sandmodels` — rev0079 / Lookout

Lookout adds two opt-in local discovery controls:

```text
/sandmodels all --recent 14
/sandmodels paid --sort newest
/sandmodels all --recent 30 --tools --sort newest
/sandmodels paid --inspect vendor/model --details
```

`--recent DAYS` keeps models whose OpenRouter catalog-added timestamp is no more
than that many 24-hour periods before the loaded catalog snapshot. `DAYS` must be
a whole number from 1 through 3650. `--sort newest` orders known catalog-added
timestamps descending. Unknown timestamps sort last. Equal timestamps retain the
existing order.

These controls do not change the default. Plain `/sandmodels` remains the free
section in its established route-aware order. No query persists.

## What the timestamp means

OpenRouter documents the Models API `created` field as the Unix timestamp when a
model was added to OpenRouter, and documents its `newest` sort as “most recently
added to OpenRouter.” Sandpi preserves that field as `created_at` after bounded
validation.

It is **not** treated as:

- the model's original release, training, knowledge-cutoff, or weight-build date;
- evidence that a provider endpoint is currently available;
- a quality, coding, latency, popularity, or price ranking;
- an instruction to replace a reviewed recipe or explicit native Pi choice;
- proof that a model added earlier is obsolete.

The UI uses “OpenRouter catalog-added” throughout. It does not shorten that to a
bare “released” or “new model” claim.

## The loaded snapshot is the reference clock

Recency is measured against the `fetched_at` value of the catalog snapshot already
loaded for this Pi invocation, not against the wall clock on every command. This
keeps a query stable during a long-running process and makes its evidence source
visible.

The report says whether it uses the free or full snapshot, gives the UTC snapshot
date, and discloses stale or failed-refresh state. A stale snapshot can describe
only the rows it contains; it cannot reveal models added after it.

Missing, malformed, implausible, or snapshot-future timestamps are **unknown**.
Unknown is neither old nor recent:

- `--recent` excludes unknowns;
- `--sort newest` leaves them selectable but places them after known timestamps;
- without a recency query, unknown and older rows remain selectable.

The exact `--recent` boundary is inclusive in seconds. A model added exactly seven
days before the loaded snapshot passes `--recent 7`; one second earlier does not.

## Local catalog projection

Catalog normalization accepts only integer timestamps from 2000-01-01 through
2100-01-01 UTC. A bad optional timestamp does not reject an otherwise usable live
catalog row; it becomes unknown. Cached snapshots reject a present malformed
`created_at` field so local cache tampering cannot turn arbitrary data into a date.

Older caches without `created_at` remain compatible and produce unknown recency.
The per-model route/capability fingerprint deliberately excludes `created_at`:
learning when OpenRouter added an otherwise unchanged row must not invalidate an
existing coding grade or route-health observation. The complete catalog snapshot
digest still covers the field.

The guest passes only this bounded model field plus a small snapshot projection:
`fetched_at`, stale/refresh-failed booleans, and whether the free or full catalog
supplied the rows. No descriptions, benchmark tables, endpoint lists, credentials,
or arbitrary API response objects are added to Pi runtime metadata.

## Picker, explanation, and preview behavior

The controls combine with all existing terms and filters using AND semantics:

```text
/sandmodels paid kimi --recent 90 --max-output 5 --sort newest
/sandmodels all --vision --recent 30 --explain
```

`--explain` distinguishes a known timestamp outside the requested window from an
unknown timestamp/snapshot. It never silently drops the recency filter, broadens
the section, refreshes the catalog, guesses an alias, or substitutes a successor.
Counts can overlap with tool, vision, fit, review, search, or price exclusions.

Selector rows show `added YYYY-MM-DD` when `--recent` or `--sort newest` is active.
The default overview includes catalog-added evidence only for such a query. The
`--details` report always includes the candidate's bounded catalog-added evidence,
so exact inspection can expose it without changing model eligibility.

The existing full-preview rerender remains the confirmation guard. Because the
candidate's added metadata is part of that report, a changed known/unknown/date or
snapshot disclosure can make a pending preview stale. This is not an atomic lock
over a catalog refresh, provider state, or the next outgoing request.

## No refresh, provider probe, or recommendation authority

`/sandmodels` still performs no catalog, credential, credit, account, endpoint, or
inference request. Refresh remains a separate host action:

```text
sandpi models --refresh
```

A running Pi invocation keeps the snapshot it loaded; refreshing on the host does
not silently replace its in-process model list. Start a new invocation to load the
new result.

The controls do not enable paid access, alter routing preferences, set a startup
default, choose a thinking level, change tools, compact context, or send a prompt.
Confirmation still delegates the exact registered candidate to Pi. Explicit
native `/model` and native settings remain authoritative.

## Validation scope

The release exercises normalization, cache compatibility, runtime projection,
query grammar, inclusive boundaries, unknown states, stable sorting, explanation,
preview disclosure, and actual `/sandmodels` command behavior with explicit Pi/UI
stand-ins. Exact-parent comparisons show the new behavior while preserving default
ordering and prior filters.

Not run here:

- installed Pi terminal or RPC rendering;
- a real stale-cache refresh followed by a new Pi invocation;
- native selection from a large live catalog;
- provider readiness or inference for a recently added model;
- any comparison of freshness with quality, latency, price, or task success.
