# Native continuation precedence

Rev0062 / Junction — 2026-10-08. Contract: `src/guest.py:inspect_pi_invocation`.

## Why this matters

A wrapper or shell function can include `-c` by default while its caller supplies
an explicit native session or requests the picker. Rev0061 remembered the `-c`
Boolean even when the native operation was not latest-session continuation. Its
mode label also depended on argument order. The actual publication path therefore
ran the latest-session preflight and bound that unrelated snapshot to the process.

Sandpi's unchanged TypeScript extension correctly rejects a mismatch between a
real continuation snapshot and the actual session. The defect was attaching that
snapshot to the wrong operation, not the mismatch check itself. Snapshot metadata
can include model, tool-profile and affinity facts; do not reuse it for an
unrelated target or suppress the check merely to make startup succeed.

## Behavior

These examples are inside the room's shell. Replace CHOSEN with the intended
native session path or ID. They are invocations, not universal offline diagnostics.

| Invocation | Guest lifecycle scope | Latest-session preflight |
|---|---|---|
| `pi -c` | continue | Unchanged: yes |
| `pi -c --session CHOSEN` | session | No; existing generic native guard |
| `pi --session CHOSEN -c` | session | Same, regardless of order |
| `pi -c -r` or `pi -r -c` | picker | No; existing generic native guard |
| `pi --session CHOSEN -r` | session | Explicit session takes precedence |
| `pi -c --no-session` | new | No; no history guard |
| `pi -c --help` / `--version` / `--list-models` | command | No |
| `pi -c --export CHOSEN` | command | No; native export still uses its own input |

The per-process policy uses existing mode values and schema. It collects selectors,
then resolves command/metadata, in-memory entry, fork, explicit session/ID, picker,
and finally continue. It does not record the session ID, session target, export
path, prompt text or file arguments in the invocation summary. Existing plan and
launch arguments still contain user input and are not redacted reports.

The host base describes launch intent; per-process guest preparation is the
boundary that decides whether the deterministic preflight applies. Cold launch
history does not override a secondary invocation. This revision does not change
host plan output or add another host session index.

## Native ownership and limits

Pi remains responsible for path/ID lookup, validation, interactive picker choice,
fork creation, and session restoration. Conflicting fork/session-ID combinations
still reach Pi unchanged; Sandpi does not first read the unrelated latest session.
That is not acceptance of invalid combinations. Missing session/fork values are
conservatively unresolved selections; full CLI diagnostic equivalence is not
claimed. Repeated actual session/fork/export values keep the final value, including
empty strings. An empty session-ID value still requires native validation.

Literal flags after native `--`, and flags used as required option values, are
not selectors. Equals-form long options are not promoted to native built-ins.
This patch leaves the existing lexical scanners and wrapper-flag stripping alone;
it does not claim a complete parse of addon flags, native subcommands, or invalid
guarded-value combinations. Only known native subcommand/metadata scope is handled.

No raw arguments are rewritten. Native settings, model selection, thinking,
explicit paid permission, price caps, MCP and tool selection are not changed.
The wrapper still reconciles its managed resources and writes an invocation
record. Removing an unnecessary preflight is not making the entire wrapper
read-only, nor does it guarantee no other component reads session metadata.

`--no-session` means native in-memory conversation storage, not no tools, no model
usage, no filesystem effects or no logs. It does not enable paid requests. Native
export reads the selected input and writes HTML; it is not a read-only command.
Help/listing can still load resources or perform startup operations; use native
controls deliberately rather than treating all metadata invocations as offline.

## Evidence

The 35 Python tests include intent precedence, literal/value/empty handling,
actual per-process runtime publication and four real guest CLI/exec paths. Those
executables finish in a deliberately non-Pi argument/runtime observer. A forbidden
preflight callback proves when Sandpi does not request the read; a positive
callback control preserves ordinary continuation and session-directory delivery.

Nine Node cases feed actual Python projection into the actual unchanged TypeScript
