# Advisory defaults that remain subordinate to native Pi

Sandpi rev0029 / Fieldcraft, 2026-10-06. Applies to the pinned Pi 1.0.4.

## What changed

Paid and free OpenRouter models have different context and output limits.
Sandpi already supplied model-sized compaction recommendations and selected
starting thinking preferences. It wrote them into Pi's native personal settings
and recorded the values it had inserted in a separate provenance file.

Rev0028 compared a whole compaction model row to the previously inserted row.
Editing only keepRecentTokens made reserveTokens look user-owned too. That could
leave an obsolete reserve as a model's catalog changed, or retain the stale
reserve after its recommendation disappeared. Existing ordinary personal defaults
could also be shadowed by newly inserted exact-model defaults.

Rev0029 reconciles each field independently. It does not change the recommended
numbers or model recipes. Its job is to distinguish an owned default from a user
choice, not select a supposedly universal coding configuration.

## Runtime behavior

| Settings state | Next reconciliation |
|---|---|
| Newly discovered exact model, no personal overrides | Install current recommendations and record only the fields inserted. |
| Unchanged generated field | Refresh from the current recommendation, or withdraw if it no longer applies. |
| One edited field | Keep it; continue updating the other still-owned field. |
| One field deleted from a partly managed row | Keep it absent so Pi can inherit normally; continue updating the other field. |
| Entire exact-model row deleted | Reset that row to current recommendations on the next reconciliation. |
| Preexisting user row, even partial or empty | Keep it verbatim. Never fill in fields the user omitted. |
| Explicit ordinary personal token value | Do not add the corresponding exact-model default; withdraw only the already-owned one. |
| Explicit personal defaultThinkingLevel | Do not add an exact-model recipe preference; withdraw only an already-owned preference. |
| Explicit unowned exact-model thinking/token choice | Keep it even when an ordinary default is present. |
| Recommendation disappears | Remove only still-owned fields. Keep edited fields, unknown native fields and deliberate omissions. |

A partly edited row stays marked while another field is managed. This avoids
turning a deliberate omitted field back into a generated value on a later
reconciliation. No perpetual tombstones are kept. An edited row with all owned
fields withdrawn remains a native user row, including an explicit empty object.
Deleting the complete row is the way to return it to automatic recommendations.

Only the OpenRouter recommendations are reconciled. GPT, Gemini and other
providers' native settings remain untouched. No request, context compaction,
model selection, sampling override, paid authorization, tool change or transcript
mutation is performed by this module.

## Native scope precedence is not changed

Pi first merges personal and trusted project settings, then resolves each token
field from the exact model override, the ordinary compaction setting, and finally
its built-in default. Consequently, a general project default does not beat an
exact-model value supplied in personal settings. A project must set the matching
exact-model field to replace it. This follows Pi's source, not a Sandpi merge rule.

For example, a project-local override can use this shape (replace the synthetic
ID with the actual model selected in Pi):

```json
{
  "compaction": {
    "modelOverrides": {
      "openrouter/vendor/exact-model": {
        "reserveTokens": 8192
      }
    }
  },
  "modelThinkingLevels": {
    "openrouter/vendor/exact-model": "low"
  }
}
```

These numbers are an example, not a recommendation for every model. The native
project file is `.pi/settings.json`; Pi decides whether it is trusted. Sandpi's
reconciler never discovers, reads, rewrites or trusts that file. Use /sanddefaults
to inspect Pi's effective current values and each field's source. The diagnostic
makes no network calls and saves no settings.

Ordinary **personal** settings are respected when Sandpi prepares/reconciles the
room runtime. This is not a watcher and does not promise immediate withdrawal of
an old managed value during an already-running Pi process. Re-entry performs the
reconciliation; native exact-model/project edits follow Pi's own reload behavior.

## Evidence and migration

`src/settings_defaults.py` is a pure transformation. Its inputs are personal
settings, existing provenance, and already-produced recommendations. IO remains
in `src/guest.py`. `src/nixhost.py` includes the helper in the guest closure.

The provenance file remains `<Pi agent directory>/sandpi-settings-overlay.json`.
Schema v2 contains owned compaction fields, owned exact-model thinking values,
and keys of partially edited rows that retain some managed fields. It contains
no prompt, response, tool output, credential, session ID or model history. Map
cardinality follows the catalog bound; reading retains the existing JSON byte
bound. Unknown provenance schema/fields fail with a repairable configuration
error. Invalid user values remain for Pi to validate instead of being hidden with
more-specific generated values.

Existing v1 evidence migrates on successful runtime preparation. If an earlier
release already discarded provenance for a partially edited row, rev0029 cannot
recover the lost intent and does not guess from matching numbers. Those values
remain user-owned. Deleting that complete exact-model row opts back into current
recommendations. Removing the sidecar alone does not claim ownership over old
settings.

Same-value writes are also indistinguishable from untouched generated values.
Saving a value numerically equal to the managed value does not automatically
transfer ownership. A project exact-model override is a separate native layer
and avoids this ambiguity. Do not intercept all thinking/model events and claim
they reveal whether Ctrl+S was used: the pinned selection event does not carry
that persistence intent.

## Publication and concurrent edits

Settings are atomically written before the provenance file. A settings-write
failure therefore leaves old provenance. A provenance-write failure leaves the
newly installed settings and stops preparation; a later reconciliation handles
mismatched old values conservatively rather than undoing user edits.

These are two per-file atomic publications, not a single transaction. Sandpi's
existing runtime-composition lock coordinates Sandpi composers, but is not Pi's
proper-lockfile protocol. Do not edit personal settings concurrently with room
runtime preparation and assume serializable saves. Upstream low-precedence
system defaults or coordinated settings writes would be a cleaner future seam.
This revision does not invent stronger guarantees than the existing locks.

## Tests and retirement path

The offline tests cover pure ownership, v1 migration, one-field deletions, repeated
catalog changes, complete removal/return, user empty rows, bool/int distinctions,
bounds, source closure inclusion, native-file noninterference and both IO failure
boundaries. Seeded sequences preserve edited values over repeated refreshes.

`python3 tools/acceptance.py --stage pi` additionally invokes the actual pinned
Pi SettingsManager with eight synthetic cases. It checks partial-edit refresh,
personal ordinary values, partial-row inheritance, exact project precedence,
ordinary project specificity, trust transition, empty-row native inheritance and
invalid-native-value failure. No session or inference is needed for these cases.
The test runner requires the complete exact list of successful observations.

Remove this adapter when Pi supplies a supported system-default layer beneath
personal and project settings. Keep the model-recipe table independently removable
and do not turn the provenance file into another Pi settings language.
