# Sandpi dialog coexistence — rev0072 / Courtesy

## One local decision, not an action queue

The model picker, reviewed handoff and landing-note mutations share one small
in-memory admission object per loaded Sandpi extension. The admitted workflow owns
it through its existing waits, editor, confirmation and final native call. A
second workflow is refused before opening its own dialog or inspecting task state;
it is **not queued** for later execution. Finish or cancel the current workflow,
then deliberately invoke the next one. There is no additional confirmation step.

Covered commands are `/sandmodels` selection, `/sandhandoff` (including `--blank`),
and `/sandpi note edit`, `clear`, and `draft`. Read-only `/sandpi note`, command
help, `/sandmodels --explain`, invalid-argument diagnostics and non-TUI refusal
paths remain independent. Their return cannot release another workflow's owner.
This is not a global Pi UI lock: native `/model`, `/tree`, other addons, the
other Pi processes are not serialized. As of rev0074, the terminal startup saved-model
recovery chooser also shares ownership; see `PI-RECOVERY-CHOOSER.md`.
No queued command is replayed or model chosen on another command's behalf.

## Lifetime and native UI limits

A ticket carries one AbortSignal. Select/confirm get that signal through Pi's
existing dialog options. The extension's existing `session_shutdown` handler
retires the owner before its ordinary observation work. Retire first clears the
active identity and then aborts its signal. Releasing an old ticket cannot unlock
a newer workflow, including when the old native promise settles after shutdown.

Pi's multi-line editor has **no abort option** in this pinned public API. Sandpi
does not add one, replace the editor, race it with a timer, or claim the native
editor promise has completed. It can remain pending after teardown. If that
editor later resolves or rejects, the retired command stops before using its
old context, confirming, appending a note, staging a draft or requesting a session.
A new extension runtime has independent admission. Native lifecycle guards still
own the validity of its contexts. This is not a concurrent session-file lock.

Native RPC abort removes its server-side pending request, not a guaranteed
client-visible dismissal. Native UI notifications remain fire-and-forget. TUI
rendering, input focus and client display are not established by local emission.
Do not describe a cancellation signal as a rendered-dialog acknowledgement.

## Actions already delegated keep their native outcome

Admission is not rollback. A native model change already delegated is neither
cancelled nor repeated. A handoff that has already called `newSession` can run its
existing `withSession` callback in the replacement context even though its old
runtime ticket was retired by that same transition. Checking the old ticket in
that callback would wrongly prevent the deliberately confirmed handoff draft.
The replacement's idle/queue/empty-editor checks remain unchanged.

Note append/editor writes retain their existing uncertainty reporting if native
code mutates and then throws. There is no automatic retry, rollback, deletion,
extra conversation message, or forced persistence. Note-only and ephemeral
session saving limitations still apply. A main-editor draft is not overwritten.

## Model and permission ownership

Acquiring or refusing a ticket is not an inference request. It does not query keys,
credits, catalogs, account settings, tools, transcripts or files. It never grants
paid access, compacts, switches thinking, substitutes a model or saves a setting.
The admitted command retains its ordinary native side effects: a confirmed note
appends metadata, a confirmed draft writes editor text, a handoff requests a new
session and a confirmed picker delegates a model change. Native lifecycle observers
still run. Do not call the entire command/lifecycle universally read-only.

Free-first discovery, the overview/details flag, preview guards, exact-model
selection, optional prompts/skills, model recipes and shared credentials remain
