# Native OpenRouter endpoint preferences

Reviewed 2026-10-07 against Pi 1.0.4. Sandpi rev0031.

## The useful distinction

A physical model ID chooses the model. An endpoint preference chooses which
provider serves that same model. Neither a low price nor high tokens/second is a
coding grade. This adapter does not change the model or choose an endpoint for you.

Leave routing alone initially. Sandpi does not add order, only, ignore or sort
preferences. Work normally through `sandpi`, `sandpi --paid --preset kimi`, native
`/model`, `/thinking`, `/mcp`, and `/reload`. GPT and Gemini routes are unchanged.
Free-only access, explicit paid permission, key-bank scheduling and the small-model
ladder remain separate concerns.

## Use Pi's own configuration when a preference is useful

Inside the room, edit `~/.pi/agent/models.json` (or the active custom agent
location). Merge into the existing document; do not replace unrelated providers
or overrides. No separate Sandpi routing file or new command-line option is needed.

Example for a known exact Messages model:

```json
{
  "providers": {
    "openrouter": {
      "modelOverrides": {
        "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5.5": {
          "compat": {
            "openRouterRouting": {
              "only": ["anthropic"],
              "allow_fallbacks": false
            }
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

The model ID and endpoint slug illustrate the shape; check the endpoint list for
your exact model. They are not a promise that an endpoint is currently available
or free. Use the physical model ID inside modelOverrides, not a second prefixed
`openrouter/` ID. The same compatibility key works for managed Chat models.

Pi composes these values after Sandpi supplies its managed baseline. Reload native
Pi configuration and reselect the model as appropriate, then use `/sanddefaults`
to inspect its effective configured preferences. Merely saving a file need not
replace the model object of an already-active conversation.

To return to the prior policy, remove the added routing fields from the relevant
native override and reload. Empty objects can merge with inherited compatibility
values; do not assume `{}` erases every inherited field. Sandpi does not implement
a second merge rule or claim to own your native override.

## Choosing the smallest useful preference

| Preference | Intended use | Consequence |
|---|---|---|
| `order` | Prefer endpoints in a particular order | A preference, not an exclusive allowlist. It takes priority over automatic sticky routing. |
| `only` | Restrict the eligible endpoints | Fewer routes can mean an error during an outage or when parameters are unsupported. |
| `ignore` | Exclude endpoints you have chosen not to use | Applies to providers, not a different physical model. |
| `sort` | Prefer price, throughput or latency | Changes gateway default selection; speed and cost are not coding-quality scores. |
| `preferred_min_throughput` / `preferred_max_latency` | Express performance preferences | Gateway preferences, not proof of end-to-end task performance or hard exclusion. |
| `max_price` | Limit eligible endpoint unit prices | Not a total spending budget. Free-only requests still have zero-price caps. |

`allow_fallbacks` concerns endpoints. It does not authorize Sandpi to choose a
different model. The existing final request guard still refuses model-fallback
lists and hidden model-router plugins. It also requires parameter support.

Explicit `order` takes priority over OpenRouter's automatic cache-sticky routing.
For a long coding conversation, keeping the default may be better than chasing
a small latency difference. `/sandcost` reports actual observed cache/charge data
when present; this revision neither polls for it nor promises cache hits.

## Composition order, including request hooks

1. Pi reads native configuration and builds the active model.
2. Native Chat already inserts compat.openRouterRouting in the request. For
   Messages only, Sandpi inserts it if the payload has no own provider field.
3. The caller's onPayload hook sees the prepared payload and may replace, mutate,
   or clear preferences. Replacement of the entire payload is also respected.
4. Sandpi's existing exact-model, required-parameter and free/paid price rules
   apply. Free caps stay zero. An explicit launch ceiling only tightens paid caps.
5. Pi's native transport sends its normal protocol and handles the stream.

An upstream-provided provider field always wins over the compatibility shim,
including an explicit null or undefined field. A hook deleting preferences does
not cause them to reappear after the hook. The mandatory final constraints are
not considered user endpoint preferences and therefore remain.

