Introduced in **rev0034 / Scopeglass**, targeting pinned Pi **1.0.4**.

A useful multi-key scheduler must receive the same failure scope whether Pi
hands it an object or a serialized error envelope. This revision corrects that
representation boundary; it does not add another scheduler, retry loop or model
policy.

## Rev0053 credit refinement

`PI-CREDIT-FAILURES.md` adds exact billing sources and bounded subordinate reasons.
Single-request weight rejection now uses neutral request handling, not group quota;
unknown explicit sources no longer inherit a guessed substring scope. Original
unscoped 402 behavior and actual typed temporary-hold behavior remain unchanged.
No discarded HTTP headers are recovered. See the newer contract for current
credit handling, and this document for the inherited envelope/provenance boundary.

## The practical difference

The exact-parent comparison in `evidence/rev0034/baseline-error-comparison.json`
uses the original rev0033 module bytes, not an approximate old implementation.
It demonstrates these incorrect decisions and their corrections:

| Evidence already provided | Before | Now |
|---|---|---|
| Serialized OpenRouter key spending limit | Entire declared account group cooled | Only the affected key is cooled |
| Serialized in-flight spending limit | Treated as exhausted credit | Existing temporary group throttling applies |
| Serialized upstream rate limit with provider_code | Entire OpenRouter bank cooled | Existing quota-group/model route cooldown applies |
| Messages canonical not_found without a numeric code | Unclassified | Model-scoped missing-route handling |
| Messages invalid_request mentioning 401 fields | Authentication failure | Request error; no key churn |
| Wait hint in serialized metadata | Lost | Supplied to the existing scheduler |

Nothing here makes additional keys increase a shared account allowance. Groups
still mean what the operator configured. There is no new account-discovery or
quota-bypass mechanism. A temporary key cooldown is not credential deletion.

## Representation contract

`src/pi/error-evidence.mjs` is an in-memory compatibility projection. It reads
known error containers and known scalar fields only. It can recognize the same
whole envelope in an SDK Error, a final AssistantMessage.errorMessage, or an
already-observed raw error event. An optional HTTP-status prefix is accepted.

JSON display input is limited to 8,192 characters. Arrays, malformed/truncated
objects, ambiguous canonical metadata, conflicting statuses and JSON fragments
inside arbitrary prose cannot authorize key failover or a guessed cooldown.
No JSON repair is attempted. Unknown canonical error types remain neutral instead
of using words in their diagnostic message to guess authentication or throttling.
A number such as 401 in a field-count message is not an HTTP status.

Documented error_type takes precedence over lossy protocol labels. Existing
camel-case aliases and case normalization are handled consistently, including at
the context-recovery boundary. A non-context canonical type cannot become
context overflow just because its message mentions the context window.
Structured evidence wins over a contradictory display string. A raw canonical
error wins over the native display representation. Cancellation wins over both.
Plain legacy errors outside recognized JSON envelopes retain their existing
classification path. Google/Gemini remains on its prior classification path.

The projection does not walk choices, messages, tool arguments, debug payloads,
flagged input or provider raw bodies. It does not mutate its input or write files.
Only the existing fixed classification, status, scope and valid wait hint reach
the host scheduler. The provider's diagnostic message is private working data;
it is not copied into Sandpi's displayed failure or a new ledger.

