## A natural stopping point for long work

Inside Pi, request a brief in the conversation that knows the work:

```text
/sandbrief implement the next parser stage and retain the compatibility constraints
```

This is an ordinary native prompt template, not a local diagnostic. Submitting it
starts a normal Pi turn with the selected model, current context, reasoning, tool
configuration, cancellation, provider wrappers and billing policy. It asks for a
250-500 word working-set brief (less when sufficient), with a concrete task,
constraints/non-goals, relevant file pointers and first-read anchors, evidence
provenance/freshness, blockers and one bounded next step. Explicit length/scope
requests can adjust the guidance; these are not enforced limits. Pointers are not
attached files or proof of their current bytes. See `PI-WORKING-SET.md`. It asks the
model not to call tools, run checks, or do the next task. That is an instruction,
not an enforced tool sandbox. Nothing changes the native active-tool set.

When the reply has finished, run:

```text
/sandhandoff
```

A native multiline editor opens with the latest completed assistant text. Check
facts, remove sensitive content, and edit the next task. A separate confirmation
then asks to create a native new session with a parent link to the source file
when one is available. Only a fixed introductory instruction plus your reviewed
text is placed in the replacement editor. It is NOT automatically submitted.
Review the new session's model and paid policy before pressing Enter.

There is no hidden second summarization call, remote export, host-side transcript
reader, custom session file, background agent, or handoff database. The source is
not compacted or erased. A saved source session can be selected using `/resume`.
An unsaved/in-memory source may not be recoverable after leaving it. The new draft
is not a conversation message until submitted; do not depend on this command to
persist an unsent draft. Cancelling a dialog discards edits made inside that dialog.

For a manual brief without any summarization request:

```text
/sandhandoff --blank
/sandhandoff --help
```

Blank mode needs neither a model nor an existing answer and now uses the same
working-set outline. It does not validate factual completeness. Replace the placeholders
before confirming. The command also accepts an ordinary completed reply; it does
not pretend to authenticate that the reply originated from `/sandbrief`.

## Deliberately narrow defaults

The handoff transition is terminal-only. Native RPC exposes `editor` and
`set_editor_text`, but cannot read the client's unsent editor and does not
acknowledge draft installation. This revision declines RPC transitions rather
than guessing that such a draft is empty. Help is available in every mode.
Normal prompt-template use remains governed by Pi's mode and input processing.

The command refuses active/queued work instead of aborting it or scheduling a
later transition. An existing unsent main-editor draft is never replaced. A
changed session, leaf, model identity/transport, source projection, work queue,
or main-editor text invalidates an open handoff. Re-entry cannot create two
simultaneous handoff dialogs. Native session-replacement vetoes are respected.

After successful replacement only Pi's `withSession` context is used. If another
extension has already populated the new editor or started work, Sandpi does not
overwrite it. A warning says that the handoff draft was not installed and dialog
edits were not saved. Transition errors do not claim the source is unchanged when
native replacement may already have occurred. Exception bodies are not printed.
This is defensive integration, not a transaction across every arbitrary addon.

Pi retains ownership of new-session defaults, model, thinking, tools and startup
hooks. Handoff does not force the source model onto the replacement or persist a
new default. It never enables paid access or silently chooses a free substitute.
For OpenRouter, `/sandbrief` and `/sandreview` use the existing free/paid policy;
`/sandhandoff` itself calls no provider, key service or credit endpoint.

## Exactly what is carried

The source is Pi's `buildSessionProjection()`, not raw session-file text or a
hand-built compaction algorithm. Native compaction and context edits therefore
own what is available. The command stops at the latest conversational entry.
If that reply was removed, cancelled, truncated, errored, or interrupted by a
new user/tool/custom message, an older successful answer is not substituted.

Only a completed `stop` assistant message with text/thinking blocks is accepted;
only text blocks are carried. Hidden reasoning and signatures are never copied.
Images, audio, tool calls, malformed text, and unsupported blocks cause a refusal,
not a silent text-only export. This does not include the full transcript, prior
tool output, or independently observed verification evidence. The brief may
contain mistakes and secrets; reviewing it is not automatic validation/redaction.

The body is bounded to 32,000 JavaScript string code units. This is a UI bound,
not a token estimate or model-context-fit guarantee. Oversize/empty text and
terminal control characters are rejected rather than silently rewritten. A fixed
leading instruction ensures that a brief starting with `/command` or `!shell`
becomes ordinary prompt text, not a top-level native command on submission.

## An evidence-first review before the handoff

```text
/sandreview
/sandreview the migration path and cancellation behavior
```

This second native template requests read-only inspection, findings first, a
verification ledger, and a readiness judgment tied to evidence. Without a narrower requested scope it includes staged, unstaged and relevant
untracked work without assuming every change is this task's. Rev0068 also supports
reviewing only supplied briefs, images or excerpts, without additional collection;
see [scoped review](PI-SCOPED-REVIEW.md). It distinguishes observed results, earlier claims, and unrun checks.
It asks for no edits, commits, resets, stashes, dependency installs, test execution,
credential-store access, or additional agents. It proposes the smallest useful
next verification step rather than claiming to have run it.

This is an ordinary model turn and may consume paid usage. These instructions do
not make the process read-only or change its permissions. A model's review is not
an independent audit or proof that a project is correct.

