# Pi request-header ownership

Sandpi 0.42.0 / Keyline; Pi 1.1.0.

## Contract

For Google and OpenRouter requests managed by Sandpi's shared Sandcodex credential
banks, the credential leased for that request is the only credential Sandpi passes
to the native Pi adapter.

The wrapper removes these case-insensitive header names from both the composed
request headers and the `Model.headers` object delegated to Pi:

```text
Authorization
X-Api-Key
x-goog-api-key
```

It does **not** remove ordinary headers. Attribution, tracing, routing, client
identity and user-defined non-credential headers continue through Pi's native
adapter. Examples include `HTTP-Referer`, `X-OpenRouter-Title`, `X-Title`,
`x-goog-api-client`, and arbitrary trace headers.

The public model identity, API, context limits, pricing, compatibility metadata,
input modalities and request payload are unchanged. A model object is cloned only
when one of the managed credential headers is present.

## Why both layers must be filtered

Pi 1.1.0's high-level Models request path composes provider authentication,
`model.headers`, explicit request headers and a final `transformHeaders` callback.
Sandpi then deliberately invokes the provider's native stream function with a
request-local leased key.

The native OpenAI-compatible, Anthropic Messages and Google adapters construct
clients themselves. In doing so, they merge `model.headers` again with request
headers. Filtering only the already-composed request headers therefore is not
sufficient: a credential-shaped model override can reappear after the shared-key
lease has been selected.

Keyline filters the narrow credential set from both places before delegation. It
keeps using Pi's native protocol adapters and does not add an HTTP proxy or wire
translator.

## Provider meaning

OpenRouter authenticates API calls with an API key in an `Authorization: Bearer`
header. Google documents `x-goog-api-key` as the native Gemini REST API-key
header. Pi-compatible and third-party gateways may also accept `X-Api-Key`, so it
is treated as credential-bearing on Sandpi-managed dispatch.

This is request composition, not a claim that the VM hides secrets from guest
root. Pi and code running as root in the agent-owned guest can use the inference
allowance granted to the room. Sandpi's host credential service keeps the
canonical files shared and schedules keys across processes; it does not make an
untrusted guest unable to consume the leased request.

## Native configuration remains useful

A `models.json` override may still set non-credential headers for a physical
model. Keyline intentionally preserves them. It also leaves credentials for
providers that Sandpi does not wrap alone.

For a Sandpi-managed Google or OpenRouter request, placing a different credential
in a model override is not a supported way to bypass the shared bank. Use an
explicit native provider outside the managed provider ID when a separate
credential domain is truly intended.

## Failure and retry behavior

Header filtering happens before each native dispatch, after admission and key
lease. It does not add an inference attempt, alter failover rules, change the
no-replay boundary, or create a cooldown. The leased key remains request-local and
is released through the existing scheduler outcome.

A malformed or absent `headers` object is treated as having no headers. Header
names are matched case-insensitively. Header values are never logged or copied to
acceptance receipts.

## Acceptance

The installed-Pi synthetic acceptance stage installs deliberately stale
credential headers in an exact model override, plus a non-credential canary. It
requires both native OpenRouter protocols, free and paid operation, and every
safe key attempt to:

1. authenticate with the currently leased canonical key;
2. omit all stale model credential values; and
3. preserve the non-credential model header.

The exact-parent comparison demonstrates that rev0041 removed credential headers
from request options but still delegated the original model containing credential
headers. Rev0042 removes them for Chat Completions, Anthropic Messages and Google,
while two non-credential/headerless controls remain byte-for-behavior equivalent.

## Removal criterion

Keep this compatibility shim while Sandpi wraps native stream functions with a
leased key and the native adapters independently merge `model.headers`.

It can be removed when the pinned Pi integration provides an upstream-supported
request credential override that is final for native dispatch, or when the
adapters no longer re-merge model credential headers after the Models-layer
transform. Remove it only after native Chat, Messages and Google parity tests prove
that leased credentials remain final and non-credential headers survive.
