# Request hooks: local failures, not provider failures

Applies to rev0036 / Causeway, with Pi 1.0.4 pinned unchanged. This contract
complements `PI-OBSERVER-BOUNDARY.md`; it does not make all callbacks advisory.

## Why this boundary exists

A Pi extension can inspect or replace a request in `onPayload`, inspect its
HTTP response in `onResponse`, or observe parsed stream events through
`onProviderStreamEvent`. Those callbacks are awaited by supported native adapters.
An exception may be converted into a native assistant error just like an
inference failure. See the dated primary-source references in
`RESEARCH-REV0036.md` for the exact pinned contracts.

The distinction matters when a callback itself calls another service or throws
an HTTP-looking error. In the exact rev0035 comparison, a payload or response
callback saying authentication failed caused three key leases rather than one.
A payload-hook failure made no synthetic HTTP request, but could still cool all
three keys. A response-hook failure could repeat an already accepted generation.
A hook saying context_length_exceeded could ask Pi to compact the conversation.
Those are local integration failures, not evidence about the inference provider.

## Rev0036 behavior

The existing wrapper records a fixed hook name in attempt-local memory before
rethrowing its failure to Pi. Whether the adapter throws or produces an error
terminal, that provenance wins over diagnostic text. No new provider field,
settings file, session entry, cooldown scope or host ledger is involved.

| Source of failure | Outcome |
|---|---|
| `onPayload` rejects | Stop payload preparation; close this request; no key rotation. |
| `onResponse` rejects | Close this request after response acceptance; no key rotation. |
| `onProviderStreamEvent` rejects | Preserve earlier output, close locally, never replay. |
| Actual caller/deadline cancellation | Preserve canceled status and existing cleanup. |
| Genuine provider authentication rejection | Retain the existing pre-output failover rules. |
| Genuine pre-output context overflow | Retain Pi's native context recovery signal. |

Leases still release exactly once and the release is awaited. Local hook failures
use the already supported neutral `request` outcome. HTTP status, retry delay and
cooldown scope are not attributed from a failing callback. In the host scheduler,
this outcome neither creates a cooldown nor heals a real existing cooldown.

The final diagnostic identifies `onPayload`, `onResponse`, or
`onProviderStreamEvent` and says the failure was local. It does not guess the
extension filename, copy a stack, expose credential values, or echo the raw
exception. Inspect the extension providing that hook rather than replacing a
working API key. A composed hook chain is still one hook from the wrapper's view.

## Semantics retained

Successful callbacks receive the same objects, receiver and arguments, and their
return values keep the same meaning. Payload replacements and in-place edits
still run before existing routing/price constraints. Request hooks remain
awaited; a stalled hook is handled by the existing cancellation/deadline path,
not ignored like optional diagnostics.

The marker belongs to one attempt. Normal error classifications remain unchanged
for genuine upstream failures. A real auth rejection followed by a local hook
failure cools the genuinely rejected key, then stops on the local failure; it
does not try a third key. A later invocation starts with no hook-failure marker.

Partial native content and opaque signatures survive in the existing detached
failure snapshot. Completed external tool effects are not rolled back. A partial
tool call is not promoted to an executable successful terminal by this wrapper.
A nonconforming producer that catches a failed hook and later emits `done` still
cannot claim local success; the accounting observation remains incomplete.

Already-received cost/usage observations remain observations. A response-hook
failure can happen after the remote service accepted the request. Local closure
is not proof of zero cost, refund, or remote cancellation. Missing usage is still
unknown. The wrapper does not poll billing or add an inference request.

## Scope and composition

The correction covers the existing managed Google wrapper and free/paid
OpenRouter Chat Completions and Anthropic Messages. Native GPT transport is
unchanged. Pi addons still execute inside the room. Nothing here introduces
network filtering, key-bank changes, model ranking, or another agent harness.

Rev0035's advisory boundary remains separate: warning, outcome and accounting
observers cannot block accepted completion; request hooks can. Do not generalize
the new helper to swallow all extension errors or suppress process exceptions.

## Maintenance and testing

The production change is private to `src/pi/pool.mjs`. Remove the local marker
when the pinned upstream adapter exposes equivalent documented provenance and
these tests continue to pass. Do not depend on recognizing an English phrase in
a future provider error.

The offline suite has 143 request-hook cases, including thrown/native-flattened
errors, async rejections, cancellation, argument semantics and genuine-provider
controls. The exact-parent script has sixteen cases with twelve corrections and
four preserved controls. Python also applies actual JS outcomes to actual
KeyPool state with an unrelated seeded cooldown.

The separate installed-Pi acceptance fixture uses real ModelRuntime and native
adapters with synthetic fetch. It expects 104 cases, 80 HTTP requests, 112 leases
and releases, and no external requests. Both the event iterator and result promise
must settle; Pi's retry/overflow predicates must remain false for local failures.
`evidence/rev0036/README.md` describes executed categories without conflating them.
