# Host tool intent: literal text is not configuration

Rev0059 / Accord, 2026-10-08. Pi remains 1.1.0 at
`abe508e1b89912adde45528136c3221eb69acdd7`.

## The correction

Rev0058 repaired the guest extension-bootstrap scan. The host still searched every
raw Pi argument for tool flags. It could classify a flag-shaped prompt, name or
resource path as a real tool selection. That either disabled Sandpi's managed
profile before entry, or rejected an explicit classic/codemode profile as conflicting.
The guest's correct literal-text inspection could not restore a profile the initial
host runtime had already disabled.

Rev0059 makes host tool-intent inspection use the same token boundary as guest
bootstrap. An unconsumed native `--` ends recognition. Required native values hide
flag-shaped text. Genuine native tool flags keep their prior ownership semantics.
No command, dependency, settings format, runtime module or provider request is added.

For example, on the host:

```sh
sandpi --tool-profile classic -- -- --tools
sandpi --tool-profile codemode -- --append-system-prompt --no-tools
sandpi -- --tools read,bash
```

The first `--` belongs to Sandpi and forwards the remaining argv to Pi. In the
first example, the second `--` belongs to Pi and makes `--tools` literal prompt
text. It no longer conflicts with the requested classic profile. In the second,
`--no-tools` is the value of the native append-system-prompt option, not a tool
switch. Pi owns whether a supplied native prompt value resolves as text or a path.
The third really selects tools, so ordinary native passthrough remains authoritative.

These launches are not local diagnostics: starting/submitting an ordinary Pi prompt
can use a model and its normal billing/recovery path. To inspect the host plan
without starting the room or Pi, use the existing `plan` command:

```sh
sandpi plan --tool-profile classic -- -- --tools
```

`plan` reads local host configuration/metadata and prints the plan, including the
supplied arguments. Do not share its full output without reviewing paths and prompt
text. It is not a native Pi resource-loading or provider-readiness check.

## Exact scope

The recognized tool spellings are `--tools`, `-t`, `--exclude-tools`, `-xt`,
`--no-tools`, `-nt`, `--no-builtin-tools`, and `-nbt`, in actual option positions.
Equals spellings such as `--tools=read` and `--exclude-tools=write` are not built-in
tool syntax in the pinned Pi parser. Sandpi no longer promotes them into tool
ownership; their bytes remain untouched for native diagnostics/extension flag
