Pi remains 1.1.0 at `abe508e1b89912adde45528136c3221eb69acdd7`.
This revision corrects a room-entry vocabulary mismatch and the host's narrow
native-agent interface projection. It adds no command, daemon or settings store.

## A second terminal should be able to enter the room

The host's ordinary interactive plan uses `interface: tui`. The existing v1 room
lease schema uses `interface: text` plus `interactive: true`. Rev0059 handed the
first spelling directly to the second validator, which rejected a normal secondary
Pi entry before SSH. Earlier reentry checks mostly used shell, exec or redirected
Pi paths and did not exercise the plain terminal plan with the real lease writer.

Rev0060 translates exactly `tui` to `text` at lease publication. The host plan
remains `tui`; the lease keeps its existing fields, schema and allowed values.
Unknown interfaces remain rejected. The old reader can inspect the new record,
including while draining the room; no migration, new enum or lost live entry is
required. The alias is only a publication boundary, not an invitation to store
arbitrary native interface names in old lease files.

Usual host entry remains:

```sh
sandpi                  # initial entry; or another entry into the running room
sandpi --RESUME         # existing native latest-session behavior
```

This correction does not synchronize conversations or choose a session for another
terminal. Owner-terminal and secondary/detached exit roles remain distinct. Leaving
a secondary entry still does not stop the room. The local tests establish admission,
lease visibility and exact command generation, not a real SSH/VM/terminal run.

## The host and Pi must agree about the interface flag

Use spaced native mode spellings:

```sh
sandpi -- --print "Review this change"
sandpi -- --mode json "Review this change"
sandpi -- --mode rpc
```

The first separator belongs to Sandpi and forwards the remaining arguments to Pi.
These are ordinary launches, not offline diagnostics. Submitting prompts can use
the selected model and its normal authorization, quota and billing paths.

Pinned Pi recognizes the built-in `--mode` followed by `text`, `json` or `rpc`.
The equals spelling `--mode=rpc` goes through its unknown/extension-flag path, not
its native mode branch. Rev0059 treated it as RPC anyway, selecting non-PTY
transport and an exit-with-Pi flow. Rev0060 does not promote equals spellings.
Original arguments still reach Pi unchanged; an unrecognized option can still be
a native error. This is not newly supported RPC syntax. Other extensions remain
responsible for their own flag behavior; the host does not discover or execute them.

The narrow projection now reuses `core.pi_option_indices`, already used by tool
intent and guest extension bootstrap. Required native values—including `--export`
input—hide flag-shaped tokens. An unconsumed `--` stops scanning; a `--` consumed
as a required value does not. Mode, theme and TUI-mode guarded values follow the
shared boundary. No paths are opened, prompt text parsed, credentials resolved,
arguments rewritten or new logs introduced by this helper.

Two examples of literal inputs, not native mode/print controls:

```sh
sandpi -- -- --print
sandpi -- --name --print
```

The first gives Pi literal `--print` prompt text. The second supplies that text as
the session name. The interface projection alone does not certify that all other
options, model choices, resource paths or commands in a launch are valid.

## Malformed real modes do not become plausible plans

Every real `--mode` must have a valid value before the host plans transport. A
missing value is no longer treated as the default. An earlier invalid mode cannot
be hidden by a later valid one. Repeated valid modes retain last-value semantics.
Errors preserve the existing status 64 and static diagnostic; the rejected value
is not echoed. Native Pi still diagnoses unrelated errors. This is intentionally
not a second comprehensive CLI validator or a model/tool/session argument rewrite.

Native subcommand dispatch and early-exit metadata/export behavior are not redesigned.
The projection describes the agent I/O contract; it does not predict all early
returns in Pi. Correctly ignoring a flag-shaped export input does not mean an export
starts a terminal UI or that its file exists. Likewise, leaving a following print
flag visible after an invalid theme flag does not make the theme argument valid.

