only matching native custom data is inspected; message bodies are never read.

There is no additional file, project setting, host index, journal, global fallback,
or note cache. Reload reconstructs the view from native entries. Removing the
feature does not delete existing entries. No cleanup or automatic migration runs.

## Persistence is native, not promised

A successful in-memory append is **not a durable-save receipt**. In pinned Pi,
new-session metadata may remain unflushed until a user or assistant message is
appended. A configured session filename does not establish that the file exists
or was fsynced. A note-only new session can be lost on exit. With `--no-session`,
notes are memory-only and disappear on exit/replacement. No fake message, forced
flush, inference, or external backup is added to overcome this limitation.

The UI discloses this before confirmation and after recording. Native filesystem
or entry-appended observer failure can occur after in-memory mutation. A failed
append is therefore reported as potentially present; inspect before retrying.
No automatic retry or rollback occurs. The code verifies observable native
entry identity, parent, custom type and payload, not the disk or a network copy.

Native copies/exports and tools/addons with filesystem access may expose notes.
Exclusion from normal model context is not secret storage, redaction, a trust
boundary, or a prohibition on an addon intentionally using the data later.
The user-written label describes this workflow, not authenticated authorship;
other code or hand-edited history can create matching entries. Don't store
credentials. Clearing cannot erase earlier copies or exports.

## Deliberate edits, unchanged drafts

Reading works as a local notification or stderr in non-UI handler contexts; it
does not wait for active work. Edit/clear are terminal-only. RPC does not expose
the client's real editor draft reliably, so mutations are declined there even
when dialog-capable UI is advertised. Native headless dispatch itself remains
Pi-owned; local handler tests do not prove every mode's command support.

Mutations require idle state and no pending messages. They do not wait, abort,
queue work, or replace a user's nonempty draft. A single dialog sequence may be
open in this handler. After each awaited dialog, the code rechecks manager,
session ID, branch leaf, session file, working directory, latest note, idle/queue
state and the original main-editor draft. A change abandons the proposed write.
This is a stale-dialog guard, not an atomic lock or proof against edits that change
and return to the same values. Pi's native stale-runtime protections also apply.

Cancellation, an unchanged edit, clearing an absent/cleared note, invalid input,
or stale state appends nothing. Blank editing is not clearing. Confirmation must
return Boolean true. One confirmed change calls native appendEntry once. No model,
provider, authentication, cost, tool, paid permission or global default is changed.

## Explicit bounds

Text is at most 2,000 Unicode code points (not tokens, bytes, graphemes or rendered
columns). Newlines and tabs are allowed; terminal control characters, specified
directional controls and unpaired surrogates are rejected. No silent truncation
or whitespace normalization changes saved text. The card shows at most 180 code
points after single-line newline/tab normalization, followed by a truncation
marker when needed. Native terminal wrapping/rendering remains unmeasured.

The reader visits at most 4,096 active parent links. Broken links, cycles, missing
native methods, malformed latest records, extra/future payload fields, or the
scan limit yield **unknown**, never a claimed empty note or a revived older note.
An unknown read refuses mutation rather than appending over uninspected state.

