## Purpose

A native session choice should not trigger automatic free-model qualification.
Literal prompt text should not accidentally turn that qualification off, claim an
exact model, or disappear when a model is selected. This revision completes a
narrow model/history boundary using the lexical helper already shared by tool
intent, interface selection and extension bootstrap. It is not a new CLI parser,
conversation manager or recommendation system.

## Native session identity remains native

With `SESSION_ID` set to the exact project-session UUID you intend to use:

```sh
sandpi openrouter -- --session-id "$SESSION_ID"
```

Pinned Pi opens that exact project session if present, otherwise creates one with
that ID. Sandpi now treats the spelling as possible history: bypass automatic
coding qualification/selection, publish the existing generic resume marker, and
let Pi resolve the actual conversation. `sandpi plan openrouter -- --session-id
"$SESSION_ID"` reports the ladder bypass without opening a session.

There is no host lookup, history export, ID normalization or new snapshot. The ID
is not copied into the small invocation-policy record. Existing plan/launch argv
still contain it; this is not a claim of globally redacted arguments.

The existing extension-time session projection guard handles the published marker.
No native --continue preflight is added for --session-id. If the session is new,
there may be no saved model to compare. With --fork, --session-id is the target ID;
the existing fork mode is retained in either argument order. Native Pi still
validates UUIDs, conflicting flags and whether a fork target already exists.

An explicit model override still takes precedence under existing startup policy.
This correction is not a proof of exact native session restoration or a promise
that a particular provider remains available. It does not authorize paid access.

## Literal flag-shaped arguments

```sh
sandpi openrouter -- -- --model
sandpi openrouter -- --append-system-prompt --models
```

The first `--` belongs to Sandpi. In the first example the next `--` belongs to Pi,
making `--model` a literal prompt argument. In the second, `--models` is consumed
as the append-system-prompt value. Neither is a genuine model-selection option.
The examples are real launches, not offline checks: native prompt/model usage
still applies. Whether append-system-prompt treats its supplied value as a file
is Pi's decision; Sandpi does not open or evaluate that value here.

The host now detects model/provider/scope and history flags only at candidate
native option positions. Required native values consume even flag-shaped tokens;
a consumed `--` is a value, while an unconsumed one ends option recognition.
Earlier genuine flags remain genuine even if later arguments are literal.
Only the native spaced model flags are recognized: `--model=...` stays in Pi's
unknown/extension-flag path, unmodified, and no longer supplies an exact-model
cooldown-probe marker.

Presence of a genuine model/history flag conservatively suppresses automatic
selection even when its value is missing. Sandpi does not fill a malformed native
argument by selecting a model. Pi remains the validator; this is no claim about
all native subcommands, unknown addon flags or native early exits.

