# Exact model inspection — rev0073 / Lens

`/sandmodels --inspect MODEL_ID` compares an exact managed OpenRouter candidate
without starting a model-selection interaction. No new top-level command, model
resolver, endpoint probe, history store or saved preference is introduced.

## Usage

Inside a Pi invocation with Sandpi loaded, replace these fictional IDs with IDs
from its managed catalog:

```text
/sandmodels --inspect vendor/model:free
/sandmodels paid --inspect vendor/model --details
/sandmodels all --inspect=vendor/model --tools --fits
/sandmodels all --inspect vendor/model --explain
```

The default section stays **free**, regardless of paid permission. Use `paid` or
`all` deliberately for a priced ID. The existing host `sandpi --paid` path loads
priced discovery **and enables paid access for that entry**; it is not a read-only
catalog diagnostic and is not run by inspection.

The value is one exact, case-sensitive OpenRouter model ID. It follows the existing
managed-catalog lexical boundary (1..256 ASCII characters, alphanumeric first,
then alphanumeric or `._:/+-`). It is not lowercased, prefix-stripped, treated as a
wildcard, resolved through names/recipes, or substituted with a successor. An extra
Pi provider prefix changes the ID: `vendor/model` and `openrouter/vendor/model`
are different strings. Both spaced and equals spellings work. Duplicate, absent,
empty or invalid values fail with static diagnostics, without echoing invalid data.
The existing 512-character single-line and 32-token query limits remain in force.

Other search words keep their ordinary case-insensitive AND matching; all filters
remain active. The exact ID is an additional constraint, not an override of them.
For example, `--fits` can exclude the named model when context is unknown. A blocked
free route, missing registration, list gap, outside-section ID or failed filter
produces the existing discovery explanation, not an unfiltered comparison. An
unmanaged registry-only model is not silently admitted. Unknown catalog membership
cannot establish why the ID is absent upstream.

After successful parsing, `--help` takes precedence. `--explain` with `--inspect`
returns discovery information for that exact ID, not its model comparison. Neither
reads projected images/thinking inputs just to explain exclusions. Completion
suggests the flag and existing flags, not guessed model IDs or a price threshold.

