# Advisory observers must not control inference

Introduced in rev0035 / Steadyhand. Pi remains pinned to 1.0.4. No operator flag,
model recipe, new state file, background service, or provider request is added.

## The defect

The managed wrapper distinguishes provider work from advisory UI and usage
observations, but its failure boundary did not consistently enforce that distinction.
A lease-release RPC failure emits a warning. If that warning threw (for example,
from an unavailable terminal context), an already accepted native `done` could
fall into the request-error path. The lease had already been reported as success,
but Pi received an error instead of the answer. A separate warning failure could
prevent an otherwise eligible pre-content key retry.

Synchronous try/catch also did not consume rejected promises returned by an
optional callback. And the outcome observer received the same mutable decision
object that the wrapper subsequently used for key failover.

`evidence/rev0035/baseline-comparison.json` documents eight changed scenarios and
four preserved controls against exact parent modules. These are reproducible
Sandpi defects, not claims about current provider uptime or upstream Pi bugs.

## Scope of the correction

The existing `src/pi/pool.mjs` now uses one private `advisory()` function at three
integration boundaries: optional warnings, optional outcome notifications, and the
existing synchronous usage-capture factory/sink. It catches synchronous throws
and consumes returned promise or thenable rejections. It never awaits optional
work, and it does not globally suppress unhandled errors.

Callbacks still run synchronously when called; this preserves the existing native
session-entry ordering. `captureUsage()` must synchronously return a sink function.
A promise returned by that factory is not a delayed sink: it is ignored after its
rejection handler is attached. A late factory resolution cannot acquire a fresh
session and misattribute an earlier request. Existing session/branch capture and
unattached-coverage handling in usage.mjs are unchanged.

Outcome observers receive a shallow copy of the flat classification. Changing its
`retry` or `outcome` fields cannot change the private decision or the already-issued
host release. This is an API ownership rule, not isolation of arbitrary extension
code; Pi and extensions still run inside the agent-owned machine.

## What does not change

Native `onPayload`, `onResponse`, and `onProviderStreamEvent` callbacks are NOT
routed through this helper. They are awaited in the existing request pipeline;
failures and cancellation still propagate. Pi's higher-level
`provider_stream_event` extension notification has its own upstream error policy;
this revision does not alter it. Do not treat an advisory wrapper as permission to
swallow every error in a provider hook.

The broker release is still attempted once and awaited before terminal delivery
or pre-content failover. It uses the existing bounded production RPC. Failure to
record a release is not called success for the broker: the existing warning and
lease-expiry fallback remain. The warning failing does not falsify the model's
already-completed answer. No second inference request is created by a diagnostic
failure.

Successful native events and messages keep their object identity. Signed reasoning,
text, tool-call arguments, usage, `length` and `toolUse` completions are not cloned,
rewritten, normalized, or reparsed by the correction. Existing cancellation of an
unfinished request still detaches its partial failure snapshot; that separate
behavior is unchanged. Accepted completion remains completion if a later cancel
arrives during lease bookkeeping.

A broken usage sink can still lose advisory coverage. Preserving the answer is not
proof that every charge was recorded or that remote billing stopped. `/sandcost`
remains an observation report, not an authoritative account bill.

