`tests/node/observations.test.mjs` has 60 injected-stream cases across Gemini and
both OpenRouter chat protocols, including free/paid access. It covers synchronous
throws, rejected promises, pending promises, rejecting thenables, throwing `then`
accessors, rejected-key failover, decision ownership, accepted-completion
cancellation, native request-hook failure, and lease-release ordering. It uses a
small stream test double including a terminal result promise. The full existing
Node suites continue to check native-event object identity and signed data.

`tests/test_observer_boundary.py` validates the separate acceptance receipt and
sends an actual JavaScript wrapper release to the Python KeyPool. This does not
substitute for executing native Pi.

The installed-Pi facility includes `tests/fixtures/pi-native-observations.mjs`:
48 combinations of native Chat/Messages, free/paid, stream/streamSimple and six
observer-failure scenarios. It uses actual ModelRuntime, native adapters and
AssistantMessageEventStream, synthetic HTTP/SSE, and actual native retry/overflow
classifiers. Expected totals are 56 synthetic HTTP requests, 56 leases, 56 release
attempts, zero external fetches and zero unhandled rejections. Run it through:

```bash
python3 tools/acceptance.py --stage pi
```

Receipt validation, fixture syntax checks and injected streams are recorded
separately from execution with the installed Pi package. The current executed
checks are under evidence/rev0035.

Keep this boundary private and small. Do not add an observer scheduler, persistent
failure ledger or automatic inference replay. When replacing the wrapper with
upstream support, retain these tests of observation versus control ownership.

## Local request-hook failures in rev0036

Request hooks remain awaited and request-affecting. Their own failures are now
