## The visible change

Only one `/sandmodels` selection may be open or waiting in a loaded Sandpi
extension. A second invocation returns a local notice, not another selector and
not a queued future switch. Finish/cancel the first picker, then run the desired
query again. No new command, flag, preference, countdown or automatic timeout.

The normal commands and workflow remain:

```text
/sandmodels
/sandmodels paid --max-output 5
/sandmodels paid --max-output 5 --details
/sandmodels --help
/sandmodels --explain
```

The first command remains free-first; paid discovery still requires the existing
priced-catalog entry. Selecting a paid model never enables paid access.
The overview and details reports, all cautions, page order, filter predicates and
full-preview/routing confirmation checks are unchanged. There is no extra click.

Help, --explain, malformed-query diagnostics, and headless selection guidance are
handled before admission or without acquiring ownership. They do not release an
existing picker. Other commands are not blocked. Inspection can still fail for
its existing reasons; the gate is not an availability guarantee.

## Lifetime and cancellation

The picker acquires a process-local owner before its first wait for idle. That
same owner covers each page, preview, confirmation, subsequent idle waits and the
awaited native `setModel` call. All normal returns and thrown exceptions release
ownership in a finally block. A comparison, discovery, UI or native setter error
is not swallowed, retried or converted into success by this revision.

Each selecting invocation creates one standard AbortController. The same signal
is passed to native select/confirm dialog options. On the existing
`session_shutdown` lifecycle event (quit, reload, new, resume or fork), the
extension releases ownership and aborts the old signal before its preexisting
shutdown bookkeeping. Late results check the signal before waiting or using the
old command context. Every idle-wait return checks it as well.

An old finally releases ownership only when it still owns that exact token. It
cannot clear a replacement picker's owner after shutdown/rebinding. No lifecycle
state is written to a session, runtime JSON document, settings file or host store.
The existing shutdown observer and accounting cleanup are otherwise unchanged;
that observer can still append its existing metadata/contact the host.

Native selection has an important commit boundary: **after setModel is called,
this cancellation signal does not cancel or undo that native operation**. The
revision adds no rollback or compensation and does not claim an error proves the
model stayed unchanged. It only prevents additional delegation from a cancelled
picker before that boundary. Native authentication/model events retain their
existing behavior, including any later changes made by other extensions.

## Scope limits

This is not a global dialog manager. Another addon, native `/model`, handoff, or a
note dialog is not serialized by this gate. It does not lock all model changes,
prevent concurrent native operations, synchronize different Pi processes, or
certify that a client displayed or closed a dialog. The owner is local to the
loaded extension; it is not a cross-session task registry or an operating-system
permission boundary.

In pinned RPC, abort settles the server-side dialog promise and removes its
pending request. It does not transmit a new dismiss-dialog acknowledgement to the
client. A client can keep displaying stale UI; replying to its old request cannot
revive that removed pending request. Actual client behavior needs native acceptance.
Notifications remain outside model context and are fire-and-forget.

The native waitForIdle promise itself has no new cancellation API here. Shutdown
invalidates the picker immediately; an unresolved idle wait may remain pending
until native code settles/rejects it. A client that never answers a dialog can
leave it pending until normal cancellation or shutdown. No arbitrary timeout,
