# Pi-native profiles, packages and sessions — rev0025

Sandpi does not replace Pi's conversation engine, package loader or tool
implementations. Its reviewed integration is an immutable guest-native Pi package
that applies a small policy layer to Pi 1.1.0: shared provider leasing, model-aware
tool presentation and append-only custom session entries. The policy is advisory
convenience inside the agent-owned guest, not a host security boundary.

## Base runtime versus invocation runtime

`@sandpi/core` never treats the room's persistent launch document as the policy
for every future Pi process. The guest wrapper reads that base, inspects the
current argv, optionally runs Pi's native session preflight, and publishes a
unique `sandpi.pi-invocation.v2` record. The extension accepts only an invocation
record under `sandpi.pi-runtime.v12`.

This gives ordinary Pi semantics after the first TUI exits. Plain `pi` starts
without stale resume/model/thinking/profile intent; `pi -c` projects the exact
branch it is about to continue; `pi -r` remains interactive. Package and MCP
subcommands also receive process-local runtime facts but no conversational
preflight. Nested launches resolve the immutable base rather than copying their
parent invocation.

The record contains control-plane fields only. Prompt strings, file arguments,
system-prompt contents, extension values and transcript data are not serialized.
The extension's `/room` and `/sandpi` commands show the invocation mode so the
operator can tell whether the process was initial, plain, continue, picker or a
non-conversation command.

## Native Pi state inside a cloned room

A stopped-room clone copies the whole guest disk, so native Pi session files,
installed Pi packages, settings, MCP configuration, prompts, skills and themes
inside that disk appear in the target. This is ordinary machine-state copying;
Sandpi does not parse or rewrite Pi's session tree.

Source and target may therefore contain native sessions with the same Pi session
ID and history at the instant of cloning. That does not make them one live session:
subsequent writes occur on independent guest disks. The host observation index is
not copied and is keyed by room ID plus native session identity. The clone starts
without target-room lifecycle observations, so `--RESUME` still asks Pi to project
the target disk's own exact cwd branch rather than inheriting a source-room host
hint.

The host workspace is not part of this snapshot. Both rooms see the same live
project mount; conversation time, guest-machine time and workspace time remain
three separate things. A clone does not rewind host files, Git state or external
side effects. Use Git/worktrees or an explicit project copy when independent file
state is required.

`clone --checkpoint LABEL` copies the checkpoint's guest disk directly. It does
not restore the source room. The target still receives no source checkpoints,
host observations, logs, leases or port forwards.

## Tool profiles

`--tool-profile auto` is the default. It resolves once at launch and again whenever
Pi changes the physical model:

| Selected model | Automatic profile |
|---|---|
| OpenAI Codex / OpenAI / Google | `codemode` |
| OpenRouter with a non-provisional Pi ladder floor of at least 3 | `codemode` |
| Other, unknown or provisionally graded OpenRouter model | `classic` |
| OpenRouter catalog model advertising no tool support | `text-only` |
| A launch containing Pi's own `--tools`, `--exclude-tools` or `--no-tools` flags | `passthrough` |
| Benchmark conversation | `classic` |

`classic` removes the built-in `codemode` tool from Pi's active set while
preserving every other active tool. `codemode` adds Pi's built-in Codemode while
preserving those tools; Pi's `codemode.mode = "on"` keeps direct tools available
but presents compact script-call guidance. `text-only` intentionally exposes no
active tools for a catalog model that does not advertise tool calls. `passthrough`
restores Pi's launch-time selection once, then leaves subsequent active-tool
changes to Pi.

Pi can register or activate direct/MCP tools after extension startup. Sandpi
merges those active additions into the remembered classic baseline before a
managed reapplication. The intentionally empty text-only set never erases that
baseline. Leaving passthrough adopts Pi's current non-Codemode selection; later
model events do not overwrite an ongoing passthrough selection. Sandpi never
unregisters tools, rewrites tool implementations, or turns a profile into an
operating-system permission boundary.

```bash
sandpi gpt                              # automatic codemode
sandpi gemini --tool-profile classic   # explicit direct-tool presentation
sandpi openrouter --model VENDOR/ID    # classic unless stably qualified
sandpi gpt -- --tools read,bash        # Pi owns selection; passthrough
```

Inside the TUI, `/sandprofile` displays the current request and effective profile.
`/sandprofile auto|classic|codemode|passthrough` changes it for the current branch. The decision is appended as a Sandpi custom session entry. Pi excludes
custom entries from model context, and native tree/fork inheritance keeps each
branch's profile decision with that branch.

Sandpi's Google/OpenRouter provider registration deliberately removes image,
classifier and deferred-operation callbacks. Codemode therefore cannot use its
`models` helper as an unleased side route around the managed coding bank. Chat
requests, including compaction, continue through the wrapped native stream.

## Resume behavior

Uppercase `--RESUME` maps to Pi's native `--continue`; lowercase `--resume` opens
Pi's native picker. Pi remains the sole owner and parser of session files. Sandpi
never copies a transcript into host state and does not invent a cross-harness
session format.

For deterministic continue, the guest runs `session-preflight.mjs` immediately
before starting the Pi CLI. The script imports the exact pinned Pi package and
uses `SessionManager.continueRecent(cwd, sessionDir)`, `buildSessionProjection()`
and `getBranch()`. It therefore inherits Pi's own session migration, cwd filtering
and branch rules instead of reimplementing JSONL parsing.

The output is fixed-schema and content-free: native session ID, selected file
basename/size/mtime/digest, projected model, thinking level, profile, transport,
Gemini affinity and last explicit handoff. It excludes messages, prompts, answers,
tool calls/results, summaries, usage, cost and absolute session path. A file that
changes while hashing, an unexpected field or an invalid identity aborts resume.

Pi may resolve a missing catalog model before emitting extension `session_start`.
Sandpi compares that runtime with the preflight original. It restores the original
when registered; otherwise only an explicit override or the visible bounded
OpenRouter fallback policy can change models. Session-ID and Gemini-affinity
mismatches stop before managed inference. `/sandresume` reports the result.

A branch-local profile entry is restored on startup and tree navigation. In
addition, the guest sends the host a tiny observation after session start, tree
navigation, model selection, naming changes, full settlement and shutdown. The
host stores only:

- native session ID;
- room and canonical project path/hash;
- provider/model/Pi API identity, when present;
- fixed-set Pi thinking level, when present;
- effective tool profile and lifecycle event;
- optional native session name; and
- creation/update timestamps.

Prompts, assistant messages, tool arguments, tool output, token counts, costs and
session-file paths are not accepted by the host schema. Persisted rows are
revalidated and projected back onto the fixed schema before `sandpi sessions`
prints them. Extra fields are discarded; malformed state fails closed.

```bash
sandpi sessions
sandpi sessions --json
sandpi sessions --all --limit 200
```

The host observation is diagnostic only. `--RESUME` still asks Pi for the latest
session in its native directory. The observation can preserve Sandpi's profile
choice before Pi loads the session and can explain which room/model was last seen;
it cannot authorize a provider, choose host-root mode, recover a missing Pi file,
or prove that work succeeded. `agent_settled`, not prompt acceptance or a single
`agent_end`, is recorded as the completed automatic lifecycle boundary.

## Native transport continuity

A physical OpenRouter model has an exact Pi API as well as a provider and model ID.
Under the pinned policy, non-batch `anthropic/*` models use native Anthropic
Messages and other models use OpenAI Chat Completions. The current API is appended
as `sandpi.model-transport.v1` branch metadata and projected into the transcript-
free host observation.

The transport entry is not an instruction to the model and is never inserted into
conversation context. On the first observation of a branch it records the current
provider/model/API. If the same provider/model later appears under a different API,
Sandpi appends a new entry and notifies the operator. That event is a protocol
migration, not a physical-model handoff: `sandpi.model-handoff.v1` remains reserved
for a changed provider or model ID.

A changed API does not cause Sandpi to translate old session bytes. Pi continues to
own cross-API transcript conversion, signed-thinking preservation and tool-result
serialization. The entry only prevents an implementation change from being
mistaken for byte-identical provider continuity. `/sandroute`, `/room`, model
pickers and `sandpi sessions` report `chat`, `messages`, or the exact API name.

## Exact thinking continuity

Sandpi's `--thinking` option is a managed OpenRouter contract. It is distinct from
Pi arguments passed after `--`, and it does not alter GPT, API-key OpenAI or Gemini
thinking policy.

When present, the requested level is validated twice:

1. The host validates the exact requested model or filters automatic candidates
   against current OpenRouter catalog evidence before starting a room.
2. The guest extension validates the model Pi actually activates and compares the
   requested level with Pi's active thinking level before inference.

The second guard covers native resume, initial session restoration, conversation-
tree navigation and later `/model` changes. It is intentionally strict because
Pi's generic clamp can choose a stronger supported level before a weaker one.
A launch asking for `medium` must not silently become `high`. Unsupported effort,
provider-managed reasoning with no exact selector, mandatory-reasoning `off`, and
an unexpected Pi clamp all produce a visible pre-inference stop.

A provider-managed reasoning model remains usable when no Sandpi thinking override
was requested. The extension reports that status but does not fabricate an effort
map. Exact selectable levels and optional `off` are generated from the current
catalog definition; explicit user `modelThinkingLevels` settings remain authoritative
for the Pi UI, but a conflicting active level still cannot satisfy an exact Sandpi
launch request.

Thinking is an observation, not transcript ownership. Session hint schema v4 accepts
only `off`, `minimal`, `low`, `medium`, `high`, `xhigh` or `max`, and accepts a
strict Pi API identifier alongside provider/model identity. Lists, arbitrary
strings and extra guest fields fail closed or are projected away. The host index
cannot use this field to rewrite a conversation or authorize a different model.

OpenRouter expiration state is also carried only as model metadata. Active models
may be automatic candidates; expiring and unknown models are manual-only; expired
models are omitted from the managed provider. A session that names a now-expired
model follows the same visible missing-model handoff policy—it is never silently
revived from stale catalog state.

## Model switching and removed models

With `auto`, an explicit Pi model change recalculates the profile for the new
physical model. Explicit `classic` or `codemode` stays fixed. A no-provider
`--RESUME` compares Pi's projected saved model with the model Pi actually restored.
A visible mismatch closes the run instead of silently continuing under another
model. An explicit provider/model on resume is treated as an intentional handoff.

The host index is not used to rewrite provider history. Cross-provider message
compatibility, thought signatures, compaction and branch context remain upstream
Pi behavior. Gemini conversation-origin key affinity is stored in a separate
custom entry and follows native branches without becoming an LLM message.

## Model-scoped availability and automatic qualification

OpenRouter stream errors are classified independently from tool profiles. A typed
missing/disabled-model response records a shared cooldown for that exact physical
model. It does not poison its API key, explicit quota group or the entire
OpenRouter provider. `/sandmodels`, automatic ranking and qualified resume exclude
models while that cooldown is active.

The bounded automatic coding probe may inspect at most two previously ungraded
candidates. If one disappears during the probe and the host broker independently
records the model-scoped outcome, that candidate consumes one slot and the next
eligible candidate may be tried. A generic quota, transport or provider outage
still stops the probe. A candidate's own text is never accepted as authority that
the model route disappeared.

## Room rename and native Pi continuity

Renaming a stopped VM room does not parse or rewrite Pi's session files. The
entire persistent guest disk moves under a new host room identity, so sessions,
branches, compaction entries, model/thinking changes, package state and extension
custom entries remain byte-for-byte native Pi state.

Sandpi rekeys only its transcript-free **VM** observation rows after first proving
that the target room ID has no conflicting observation. Those rows contain room,
project, native session ID, provider/model, cwd, profile and lifecycle metadata;
they contain no prompt, response, tool call or tool output. They remain advisory
and never reconstruct a Pi conversation.

Bare-metal observations deliberately stay on the old room ID along with the
separate `bare/<id>` agent directory. A VM rename cannot claim that explicit
host-root work moved. Starting bare metal under the new name creates a distinct
explicit grouping unless the operator intentionally chooses otherwise.

The final room rename receipt records both the preflight and applied observation
plan. It is evidence about bounded host metadata movement, not evidence that a Pi
session completed work or that project files match any conversation branch.

## What the metadata does not prove

Custom profile, affinity and handoff entries are ordinary Pi session metadata in
an agent-owned guest. They support continuity and operator visibility; they do not
prove a model completed work, protect a credential from guest root, or prevent a
user/extension from editing the guest session file. Host-side provider leases and
resource/lifecycle receipts remain separate facts.

## Route-aware Pi selection without transcript ownership

The managed extension receives only a launch snapshot of secret-free route health
and refreshes the active model on `/sandroute`. It does not read provider traffic,
parse Pi transcripts, or write model health into conversation context.

`/sandmodels` and a qualified `--resume-fallback best` order degraded OpenRouter
routes after nondegraded ones. The exact saved model is restored first whenever it
still exists, even when its passive observation is degraded. An explicit picker
also retains every current free route. This preserves Pi's native session/model
semantics while reducing automatic loops onto a repeatedly failing endpoint.

Route outcomes update the in-memory view when the managed stream releases its key.
The branch-local tool profile remains a separate custom entry: route health cannot
promote Codemode, and a coding grade cannot declare a currently missing route
healthy. GPT and Gemini continue to use their frontier profile policy without the
OpenRouter ladder or route ranking.

## Rev0010 exact intent versus automatic suppression

A branch can legitimately resume an exact OpenRouter physical model while an old
model-unavailable cooldown is still active. Sandpi marks that launch for a narrow
recovery attempt. The managed provider wrapper passes the marker with each
request-local key lease; the broker ignores only the model cooldown and continues
to enforce every credential, quota-group, route and provider scope.

This does not change the branch-local tool profile, thinking level, transport or
model handoff records. It does not choose another model. Automatic `/sandmodels`
ranking, qualified fallback and the bounded coding ladder still omit a cooled
model. If the exact request succeeds and began after the failure, the model
cooldown is healed; an older concurrent success cannot erase the newer record.

The request journal is outside Pi model context. Pi `/keys recent` displays a
fixed content-free projection, but neither the model nor an extension can treat
it as authority to replay committed output or claim task completion.

## Rev0011 Pi owns the workflow

Plain `sandpi` is the primary entry. Pi owns initial model resolution, native
