Sandpi records those launch facts for diagnostics but delegates their behavior to
Pi.

A managed Sandpi profile now answers only one question: should `codemode` be in
the current loadout? The ordinary direct and MCP names are read from Pi at the
moment the profile is applied. If Pi, the user, a package or MCP exposure policy
removed a name, Sandpi does not restore it from memory. This is especially
important across `/reload`, MCP connection changes, package activation, model
switches and branch navigation.

Text-only models are the one intentional exception: Sandpi temporarily selects no
tools because the exact catalog entry cannot use them. The latest capable
Pi-owned loadout is retained across that transition and restored when a capable
model returns. Passthrough is different: it transfers loadout ownership to Pi,
and leaving it adopts whatever Pi currently selected.

Under native `--no-extensions`, Pi disables discovered, configured and built-in
extensions. The wrapper explicitly reloads only `@sandpi/core`; it additionally
loads `builtin:codemode` when an explicit `--tools` pattern matches `codemode`.
This preserves the user's requested native loadout without reviving MCP or other
extensions. Pi's matching contract is mirrored narrowly for this bootstrap check:
`*` is the only wildcard and comparison is case-sensitive.

