# Saved-model recovery chooser — rev0074 / Harbor

## Return to work without another model manager

The existing terminal `ask` fallback now pages its eligible free models and shares
Sandpi's dialog ownership. No public flag, command, dependency, settings store,
automatic model recommendation or additional confirmation is introduced.

On the host, the existing entry remains:

```sh
sandpi --RESUME
sandpi --RESUME --resume-fallback ask
```

Inside the room, `pi -c` remains the normal latest-session continuation and `pi -r`
the native session picker. These are real launches, not offline inspection commands.
The chooser appears only when the existing startup recovery policy needs a different
OpenRouter model and a terminal UI is available. It is not a new on-error inference
fallback. Ordinary successful exact restoration opens no recovery dialog.

## Five candidates per page; selection still commits

Each page shows at most five model rows and up to two previous/next controls.
Labels use the existing bounded control-normalizing display helper. If two visible
rows collapse to the same label, an ambiguous reply is refused rather than choosing
the first registration. Normalization is not automatic secret redaction.
The existing free candidate order is preserved, including degraded routes last.
Recovery labels show stable route state, not a decreasing seconds count: taking
time to choose does not invalidate a still-degraded route. Actual state transitions
(such as degradation expiring) still invalidate the old row. Other route displays
and their timers are unchanged.
Row labels are derived from the exact ID and existing transport, grade, tool,
thinking, lifecycle and route summaries. Long labels are visibly truncated by the
existing bounded terminal-text normalizer and an explicit `[truncated]` suffix,
so the entire ID or every summary may not fit. This does not transform the ID passed to Pi or inspect credentials.

**Selecting a model still commits the handoff**, exactly as in the earlier recovery
chooser. There is no extra confirmation or in-dialog comparison panel. The ordinary
`/sandmodels` overview/details flow is unchanged. Navigation is not a selection;
a reply naming a row that is not on the current page is refused.

An absent/unknown reply, stale selection, current chooser error, failed native
selection, or unavailable recovery admission follows the existing blocked-startup
path. Cancelling is not permission to continue with Pi's provisional startup model.
The wrapper/extension's existing shutdown guidance remains in effect. An exception
from the selector is now converted to blocked startup without printing the private
exception text; it is not treated as a successful recovery.

## One Sandpi decision at a time

The terminal startup chooser acquires the same ephemeral ownership object used by
`/sandmodels`, `/sandhandoff`, and `/sandpi note edit|clear|draft`. A competing Sandpi
modal command is refused, not queued or replayed. Help, `/sandmodels --explain`,
`/sandmodels --inspect ID`, and note inspection remain available. They neither
acquire nor release recovery ownership. These are handler-level guarantees; native
startup command dispatch and real terminal focus still require acceptance.

If another Sandpi workflow already owns a dialog, recovery does not displace it.
Because startup cannot establish its required model, it follows the existing failure
path instead of scheduling a surprise retry. That shutdown can itself retire the
other dialog through the ordinary native lifecycle; the chooser does not independently
cancel it or release its ticket.

Native `/model`, other addons and other Pi processes remain outside the gate. The
exact-restoration and explicit `best` paths do not acquire this new chooser ticket.
No global terminal lock, command queue, deadline or timer is added.

