## Purpose

`/sandcost since-switch` already anchors its accounting window to Pi's newest
native `model_change` entry on the active branch. Buoy can also show the optional
Pi-native label attached to that exact entry.

This supplies a small, user-written hypothesis or reminder at the same boundary as
the usage evidence without creating a Sandpi experiment marker, quality database,
second model call, or automatic judge.

## Workflow

Inside Pi:

```text
/model
/tree
/sandcost since-switch
```

In `/tree`, switch to the **all** filter so settings entries are visible, select the
relevant `[model: ...]` entry, and use the native label action. Pi keybindings are
configurable; the tree help and `/hotkeys` show the active filter and label bindings.

A useful label is short and falsifiable, for example:

```text
Hypothesis: lower latency without changing parser output; migration checks NOT RUN.
```

The report then includes:

```text
Native switch label (user-written, unverified): Hypothesis: lower latency ...
```

No label is required. Clearing the native label makes the report say `none` without
changing the switch boundary or its observations.

## Native ownership

Pi session entries have IDs. A `model_change` entry records provider and model ID,
and Pi's read-only session interface exposes `getLabel(entryId)`. Labels are Pi
bookmarks/markers stored through append-only `label` entries. The tree selector can
show hidden settings entries in its `all` view and edit a selected entry's label.

Sandpi does not add a switch-marker entry or copy the label into its own schema. It
reads only the exact boundary ID selected by the existing bounded branch scan.
The label is not a prompt, custom message, tool argument, or model-visible context.

Pi resolves labels for the target entry session-wide across the session file. The usage window
remains active-branch-only, while the displayed label is the current native bookmark
for that entry. A label changed from another branch can therefore be newer than the
branch-local observations. The report calls it metadata, not branch-local proof.

## Bounded display projection

A label is user-written and unverified. Sandpi:

- reads it at most once for one found boundary;
- accepts only Pi's string-or-undefined result;
- never falls back to an older switch when the newest boundary is malformed;
- strips Unicode control and formatting characters, including terminal escapes and
  bidi controls;
- collapses whitespace;
- displays at most 240 Unicode code points with an explicit truncation marker; and
- never prints reader exception text.

`none`, `unavailable`, and `present but invalid` remain distinct. Failure to read a
label does not erase or fabricate the usage window.

The sanitization is a display precaution, not secret detection, authorship
attestation, content moderation, or proof that a hand-edited session file is safe.
Historical copies and exports can retain older labels.

## No new authority

`/sandcost since-switch` still performs no provider/account/credential/catalog
request and no model, thinking, tool, routing, editor, note, compaction, or paid
permission change. It does not append or clear labels. Use native `/tree` to manage
them.

The report does not judge the switch. The label is not scored, submitted, or compared with task output. `/sandpi note`
remains a separate task reminder and is not read. Charges and token counters remain
incomplete local observations, not an account bill or model-quality verdict.

An armed `/sandcost watch` keeps its absolute whole-session-file threshold; reading
the narrower switch report does not redefine that notice as spend since switching.

## Failure and compatibility boundaries

Older or hand-edited model-change entries can lack a usable entry ID. Their usage
window remains readable, but their label is `unavailable`. A missing `getLabel`
method, thrown read, non-string result, or unsafe display value is likewise disclosed
without exposing raw details.

This revision needs no session migration. Existing labels remain Pi-owned. It does
not change label creation, clearing, tree navigation, model switching, or native
session serialization.

