# Native terminal preferences — rev0056 / Signal

## Rev0085 regular-mode default and copying

Sandpi now initializes an absent native Pi `tuiMode` setting to `regular`. The
VM path is nested through SSH/vsock; regular mode leaves scrollback, selection,
and the terminal's copy shortcut with the caller instead of routing mouse input
through Pi's fullscreen viewport. This is a Pi setting default, not another
terminal protocol or clipboard service.

An existing personal setting is preserved. `pi --tui-mode fullscreen` and a
Sandpi launch tail containing `--tui-mode fullscreen` remain native per-process
overrides. In both modes, `/copy` asks Pi to copy the last assistant response by
native clipboard helper or OSC 52. `Ctrl+C` remains Pi's clear/exit action. The
default does not claim that every terminal accepts OSC 52 or that a multiplexer
forwards it.

## Choose at entry; let Pi render

Sandpi now carries five explicit native Pi display variables from the calling
terminal into an interactive VM entry. It does not implement another status
emitter, notification service, capability detector, terminal identity or UI theme.

```bash
PI_HARDWARE_CURSOR=1 sandpi
PI_PROGRAM_STATUS=0 sandpi --RESUME
PI_IMAGE_PROTOCOL=none PI_TRUE_COLOR=0 sandpi
```

The cursor example requests Pi's native visible hardware cursor, useful for some
input-method editor positioning. The second disables Pi's native terminal program
status reports for that invocation. The third requests no inline image rendering
and no truecolor rendering; these are display preferences, not model capabilities.
Pi still interprets them. Unsupported terminal paths are not repaired by forwarding.

| Native variable | Carried values | Meaning owned by Pi |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `PI_PROGRAM_STATUS` | `0`, `1` | Disable native status reports, or force sending without negotiation. Unset keeps native negotiated support. |
| `PI_HARDWARE_CURSOR` | `0`, `1` | `1` requests a visible hardware cursor. `0` removes that environment request; it does not defeat an independent native setting. |
| `PI_HYPERLINKS` | `0`, `1`, `auto` | Native OSC 8 capability override. |
| `PI_IMAGE_PROTOCOL` | `kitty`, `iterm2`, `none`, `auto` | Native inline-image renderer selection. |
| `PI_TRUE_COLOR` | `0`, `1`, `auto` | Native truecolor capability override. |

Matching is exact. Unsupported strings, whitespace, empty strings, aliases,
paths and oversized values are omitted, not normalized, echoed, or interpreted as
commands. `auto` is supported only by the last three variables. Use `unset
PI_PROGRAM_STATUS` in the calling shell to return to native status negotiation.
This allowlist is transport policy, not a complete validator for Pi's settings.

For hyperlinks, images and truecolor, native Pi settings take precedence over
environment variables. Unset/auto leaves native detection in charge unless settings
say otherwise. Existing guest startup files and user code can still set preferences;
Sandpi does not erase or rewrite those. Forcing unsupported capabilities can corrupt
rendering. `PI_HARDWARE_CURSOR=0` is not a universal hide switch: Pi also has
`showHardwareCursor`. See the pinned upstream research before changing semantics.

## Program status, not another agent monitor

Pinned Pi has native OSC 7501 status reporting for working, blocked, done, error and
idle. Its payload can contain the session name, a dialog title, or the first error
line. These can themselves be sensitive. Forwarding or disabling a flag is not an
automatic redaction mechanism. Sandpi does not parse prompts, observe every turn,
write a status file, poll a service, send an alert, or append conversation messages.

The default remains native negotiation, not `PI_PROGRAM_STATUS=1`. Force only when
the complete terminal path supports it. Pi documents that tmux and screen do not
forward this protocol. Actual terminal/multiplexer support is not probed here.
Status is terminal output, not an OpenRouter inference/retention policy, budget,
privacy certification, or reliable acknowledgement that a user saw a notification.
Disabling images affects display, not whether image input is sent to a model.

## Per-entry transport

Foreground owner entry carries valid choices in the existing transient user
service's environment. Known names that this caller did not supply are explicitly
removed from inherited user-manager environment with `UnsetEnvironment=`. This
avoids a stale manager value silently acting as the current terminal's choice.
Unrelated manager environment and existing TERM/COLORTERM/LANG handling are unchanged.

The existing interactive SSH command sets only those enum values in its login
shell body, after login startup and before the original command. The original
command, quoting, working directory, entry lease and exit lifecycle are retained.
There is no SSH `SendEnv`/`AcceptEnv` wildcard, additional server configuration,
agent forwarding or additional socket. Values are visible as ordinary non-secret
process environment/command arguments; do not put secrets into these variables.

Secondary and resumed interactive entries use their own caller's choices. They do
not reuse the owner terminal's preferences or update an already-running Pi. Shells
and child processes inherit normally. No preference is added to room manifests,
launch-host files, native settings or Sandpi's runtime document.

Headless SSH and boot probes are unchanged. Headless and detached transient
services do not carry these display overrides and remove the five inherited manager
names. An interactive attachment to a detached room uses that new caller's choices.

This correction covers **VM entry only**. It does not change sudo environment
handling for explicit `--BARE-METAL` host-root execution. Inside a guest shell (or an
already authorized root shell), native `PI_PROGRAM_STATUS=0 pi` remains available.
There is no broadened host-root environment forwarding.

## Boundaries

Only `src/vm.py` changes behavior. The other runtime edits are release identity.
No generic `PI_*`, credential, provider, model, reasoning, debug-log, transcript,
editor, executable, proxy, terminal-identity or host-socket environment is forwarded
