# Thinking-aware model switching — rev0069 / Cadence

`/sandmodels` now shows the thinking inputs behind a prospective native model
switch. There is no new command, flag, setting, level resolver or automatic
reasoning choice. The same free-first search and paid-access rules apply.

```text
/sandmodels
/sandmodels paid --max-output 5
/sandmodels all --tools --fits
```

Select a candidate for the full UI report and separate compact confirmation.
This illustrative fragment uses synthetic settings, not a real account or model:

```text
Thinking inputs (not a post-switch prediction):
  Active level: off; selected exact-model default: high; merged default: medium.
  Declared reasoning: yes -> yes.
```

## What is inspected

The active level comes from native `pi.getThinkingLevel()`, not a startup flag or
catalog recipe. The two default inputs come from the existing `pi.getSettings()`
merged snapshot: exact `modelThinkingLevels[provider/modelId]` for the selected
registered model and `defaultThinkingLevel`. The model declarations come from the
current effective model and exact registered candidate's Boolean `reasoning`.

The projection touches only these named fields; it does not enumerate the model
map or open a settings file. Pi's preceding getSettings() call already produces
a copy of merged settings; this is not a claim that the native clone is bounded
by this inspector. It does not inspect other models, headers, credentials,
sampling payloads, thinking budgets, hidden thinking or message bodies for this
new section. The existing image counter and context estimate remain separate.

Only Pi's seven named levels are displayed: off, minimal, low, medium, high,
xhigh and max. A missing/undefined configured field says not configured. Invalid
values, unavailable settings/getters and malformed maps say unknown, never off.
Unknown raw values and exception messages are not printed. This is not a native
settings validator; malformed settings can still fail or behave differently in Pi.

The exact provider/ID key is case-sensitive; no glob, family or suffix inference.
Input identities are bounded to 512 characters per component for this projection.
Missing/oversized identities cannot establish an absent default. Settings
inheritance is left to Pi, not reconstructed from personal/project files.

## Explain inputs, do not choose a level

The report explains the pinned native setModel path: an exact-model default,
then the merged default, then the active level or native built-in fallback;
Pi applies model support clamping. This inspector does not execute that resolver,
calculate the clamp, or promise the final level. Scoped-cycle shorthand is not an
input to this direct setModel path. Later native/extension behavior can differ.

The current level and candidate default can differ legitimately. A candidate
exact-model default differing from a known active level receives a caution.
When the exact-model default is absent, a differing known merged default gets
the corresponding caution. Unknown exact configuration does not cause a guessed
fallback. Neither caution says higher/lower expense or predicts a token budget.

A candidate explicitly declaring no reasoning support gets a caution when the
current model declares support or the active level is known and not off. Missing
or non-Boolean capability metadata is unknown, not false. This is the effective
Pi declaration, not proof that the remote model cannot reason. Provider-default
or mandatory reasoning and later payload transformations remain separate.

Matching level names across models are not equal compute, latency, token budgets,
quality or charges. OpenRouter documents varying effort/token controls and
mandatory reasoning. Hidden thinking display is not a billing control. The
existing catalog guidance, output limits and reported accounting stay unchanged.

