# Project verification maps — rev0046 / Proofmap

## The workflow

Use the optional native skill inside Pi:

```text
/skill:sand-verify
/skill:sand-verify focus on model switching and cancellation
/skill:sand-verify save the map to docs/VERIFICATION.md
```

These arguments are natural-language instructions, not parsed modes or shell
commands. The first two ask for a map in the reply; the third explicitly asks the
model to save a map to the named file after reading existing content. No executor
or automatic file writer is added. `/sandpi help` links this workflow; its guide
is a reference, not a statement that the skill is currently loaded.

The skill asks for a small set of checks grounded in actual project entry points:
what each checks, the exact command and cwd, prerequisites, expected success,
coverage limits, cost/side effects, and evidence. It favors a focused fast check
and a meaningful native/manual gap over a generic list of every test command.

Use the map, then separately authorize a chosen check in an ordinary Pi request.
Afterward, a map update can record the result's actual scope and provenance.
`/sandreview` remains a code/evidence review; `/sandbrief` remains a handoff. The
map is the missing planning document, not a third way to start an agent or CI run.

## Evidence should retain its origin

The supporting guide keeps three dimensions separate:

| Dimension | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| Result | PASS, FAIL, NOT RUN, BLOCKED or INCONCLUSIVE, according to the check's success signal. |
| Provenance | Directly observed tool result, inspected recorded artifact, reported claim, or no evidence. |
| Applicability | Tested inputs, revision plus dirty state, environment, scope and freshness. |

An old PASS remains historical after relevant inputs change. An assistant's claim
is not upgraded to observed execution. An inspected CI log is recorded evidence,
not a run performed in this conversation or automatically authenticated evidence.
Zero collected tests, unexpected skips, cancellation, timeout or partial output
must not be collapsed into an unconditional green check. Not every property is
established by a successful suite; a mock is not a live provider.

The blank template starts with NOT RUN. No receipt, run identifier, timestamp,
command, duration, test count, cost or revision is invented to complete a field.
There is no auto-expiry engine or machine-validated evidence database: freshness
is an explicit comparison requested of the model, not a runtime guarantee.

## Inspection is not execution permission

By default the instructions request read-only source inspection and a reply.
They prohibit running project code to discover checks, including test collection,
project-script help flags, dry-run targets, environment sourcing, package hooks,
downloads, installs, and delegated work. Source text and logs are data, not new
permission. Command names and README claims are traced to definitions where
possible rather than trusted as proof of behavior or safety.

**These are model instructions, not an enforced read-only mode.** The selected
model may misunderstand or ignore them. Tool permissions and operating-system
access remain unchanged. Project trust is still native Pi resource-loading policy,
not a sandbox. Review unfamiliar project files and any proposed commands.

An explicit save request names its destination. The skill asks the model to read
an existing file, preserve unrelated work, maintain evidence provenance and stable
IDs, and avoid overwriting concurrent edits. It does not itself enforce atomic
publication or detect races. Ambiguous authority leads to a proposed edit. Saving
never authorizes a test run, changes AGENTS.md or Pi configuration, installs a
project skill, or creates a background task as part of this workflow.

