# Sandpi 0.86.0 release report — Orbit

Date: 2026-10-10

## Outcome

Orbit adds bounded autonomous work passes to the native Pi conversation. The
operator can now write `/loop "objective"` for five complete agent settlements,
or `/loop PASSES "objective"` for an explicit 1–100 settlements.

This is deliberately a Pi extension rather than a host scheduler. The same Pi
session remains responsible for conversation history, tools, retries, compaction,
model selection and provider semantics. Sandpi does not silently select a model,
enable payment, alter reasoning, change tool loadout, widen workspace authority or
create a second transcript.

## Settlement boundary

The pinned Pi 1.1.0 runtime exposes `agent_before_settle` after tool work, automatic
retry, compaction and queued continuations have completed, but before final agent
settlement. Orbit appends one visible, model-context continuation at that boundary
and requests precisely one next native pass.

The pass count therefore does not equal provider requests. Tool use, provider
retry and compaction can all make multiple requests inside a single settlement.
The user-facing help and operating contract state this explicitly so a pass count
is not mistaken for a budget or request cap.

## Failure and lifetime behavior

The loop contributes no continuation after Escape or an agent/provider error.
The incoming boundary preview is intentionally not treated as the post-draft
decision: the loop's visible custom message makes final context runnable and Pi
validates it after all handlers. An unexpected early final settlement is recorded
as a durable failed loop. `/loop stop` marks the active run as stopping,
allows the current pass to settle, and contributes no next pass. It does not
abort or cancel a continuation independently owned by another extension.

Session switch, fork, tree navigation and shutdown retire the process-local loop.
The durable branch marker cannot reactivate it: a later Pi process displays the
old running state as interrupted and makes no inference request. A malformed
newest marker fails closed rather than exposing an older marker as current.

## State and privacy

The user objective appears only in ordinary visible Pi conversation messages.
The durable custom entry, which is excluded from model context, contains a random
loop ID, status, pass counts and an objective SHA-256 digest. Status and error
messages do not print the objective, credentials, prompts from other turns,
provider bodies or model output.

`/loop help`, `/loop status`, and inactive/interrupted `/loop stop` are local and
inference-free. Objectives reject unsafe terminal control/direction-formatting
characters and are bounded to 8,192 JavaScript characters.

