as an upstream failure. This is a code-composition finding, not a claim that the
upstream parser is itself defective. [3]

**Terminal semantics matter.** Pi's EventStream resolves its final-result promise
from the terminal event while also making that event available to iteration.
Tests should consume both interfaces and insist on a single coherent terminal,
not merely assert that a wrapper loop returned. [4]

**Provider-shaped words can drive recovery.** The pinned retry policy recognizes
transient-provider wording and excludes certain account/billing failures. Its
existence is another reason not to forward an arbitrary hook diagnostic as if it
were authoritative inference-provider evidence. The new static local diagnostic
avoids recovery triggers; native classifiers are checked in staged acceptance. [5]

**Gateway failures can occur before or during a stream.** OpenRouter's streaming
and error references distinguish HTTP acceptance, streamed error reporting and
normal completion. A failure in a local response observer is not evidence that a
remote request was never accepted or that it incurred no charge. This revision
makes no promise to cancel remote billing and does not add replay. [6,7]

