## Primary-source findings

Pi's extension factory belongs to an extension runtime, not the lifetime of one
conversation. Its documentation says reload replaces that runtime and requires
fresh state after `ctx.reload()`. It also notes that some commands load extensions
without starting a session. This supports keeping our factory input available
for the whole process, not only until a session shutdown event. [2]

The pinned AgentSession implementation performs native reload and rebinds the
extension runtime. Sandpi's own factory rereads `SANDPI_RUNTIME_CONFIG` at load.
The integration defect is Sandpi's mtime-only retention, not a demonstrated Pi
upstream defect. A native reload should not need another host entry to repair a
configuration file that Sandpi itself removed. [3]

Python's exec family replaces the current process without returning on success.
Sandpi's wrapper uses `os.execvpe`, and its Nix command wrapper execs the pinned
Pi command. Linux exposes the process start time as field 22 of `/proc/PID/stat`.
Boot ID distinguishes records surviving a reboot from current process identity.
These are identity inputs, not elapsed TTLs or a claim that PIDs never repeat.
The implementation preserves uncertain observations rather than guessing exit.
