## Pi's native contract

Pi documents that interactive login saves API keys or OAuth credentials in its
authentication file. Native logout removes that stored entry but does not unset
environment credentials or remove an override from models.json. These are
separate configuration sources, so Sandpi's existing shared-bank overlay must
preserve what it displaces rather than treating native storage as disposable. [2]

The pinned storage implementation uses proper-lockfile and rereads the current
file within its own modification path. That is not the same locking protocol
as Sandpi's compose lock. This patch addresses interrupted single-writer
publication; it does not pretend a Python flock now coordinates live native
logins. No native storage implementation is copied into production. [3]

The public coding-agent exports `readStoredCredential`, a one-off raw reader;
`AuthStorage` itself is not exported at that package root. The new installed-Pi
fixture therefore uses the documented public surface visible in source rather
than an assumed export or a rewritten native parser. It does not run provider
authentication, refresh, or inference. [3,4]

## Filesystem contract and design decision

Python exposes file sync and atomic replacement primitives. On Linux, syncing
file contents alone does not necessarily persist the containing directory entry;
that requires syncing the directory too. Sandpi already does both in atomic_write.
The bug was ordering these individually durable publications, not the absence of
an atomic writer. [5,6]

Write the existing recovery overlay first. Keep a union of old recovery values
and new displacements until all native writes complete. Then prune restored
values, recognizing exact duplicates left by interrupted attempts. Do not add
another credential journal, rollback daemon, authentication format, or provider
layer. Do not claim an all-files atomic transaction or disk-hardware failure
proof from process-kill tests.

The exact-parent comparison demonstrates eight corrected scenarios and four
preserved controls. Real-process cuts and local failure injection validate the
Sandpi change, independently of historical practitioner reports. No current
model's coding competence or billing behavior is inferred from this work.

