# Rev0040 research — moving aliases and replaceable paid-model opinions

Research date: October 7, 2026. Product pin: Pi 1.0.4 at
`7c10bd4337495ee613f2224843ecdf349b80d1df`.

## Question

How should Sandpi preload sane defaults for many premium OpenRouter coding models
without allowing a fast-moving public alias or model-family guess to silently
carry an old recommendation into a materially different model?

## Findings from primary sources

### Pi remains the owner of model choice

Pi stores model/thinking changes in native sessions and exposes `/model`,
`/thinking`, scoped models, model overrides, compaction overrides, and provider
registrations. Sandpi should provide initial recommendations and diagnostics, not
an external model-state machine.

Sources:

- https://github.com/earendil-works/pi/blob/v1.0.4/packages/coding-agent/docs/models.md
- https://github.com/earendil-works/pi/blob/v1.0.4/packages/coding-agent/docs/sessions.md
- https://github.com/earendil-works/pi/blob/v1.0.4/packages/coding-agent/src/core/model-resolver.ts

### OpenRouter exposes both a public ID and a canonical revision

The public model listing currently includes `canonical_slug`, capabilities,
reasoning metadata, prices, context, output capacity, lifecycle information, and
provider data. The catalog changed during this development series; a name that is
convenient for humans is not sufficient evidence that old tuning still applies.

Sources:

- https://openrouter.ai/docs/api-reference/list-available-models
- https://openrouter.ai/api/v1/models

Current reviewed examples include exact bindings such as:

- `openai/gpt-6.1-sol` → `openai/gpt-6.1-sol-20260929`
- `anthropic/claude-sonnet-5.5` → `anthropic/claude-sonnet-5.5-20260928`
- `google/gemini-3.1-pro-preview` → `google/gemini-3.1-pro-preview-20260219`
- `x-ai/grok-4.7` → `x-ai/grok-4.7-20260916`
- `moonshotai/kimi-k3` → `moonshotai/kimi-k3-20260715`
- `mistralai/mistral-large-4-0` → `mistralai/mistral-large-4-0-20261006`

These are dated review inputs, not promises that the bindings remain current.
`sandpi presets --refresh` is the operational check.

### Provider routing and cache behavior argue against gratuitous key/model churn

OpenRouter can choose among providers serving the same physical model. Native
provider preferences, `require_parameters`, exact-model constraints, stable
session identity, and prompt caching are separate concerns. A recipe should not
invent cheapest-first/fastest-first policy or silently change the physical model.

Sources:

- https://openrouter.ai/docs/features/provider-routing
- https://openrouter.ai/docs/features/prompt-caching
- https://openrouter.ai/docs/use-cases/reasoning-tokens

### Response caching is not a harmless universal default

OpenRouter documents response caching as a separate opt-in feature. It can cache
complete responses, including tool-call responses, and is scoped by key. Sandpi
does not enable it globally in this revision. Repository work often includes
changing filesystem state, so semantic response reuse needs an explicit design
and should not be smuggled into a model recipe.

Source:

- https://openrouter.ai/docs/features/response-caching

## Design decisions

1. Advance recipe schema to `sandpi.model-recipes.v2`.
2. Require an exact `canonical_slug` and a descriptive `role` in every row.
3. Bound review windows to at most 90 days; ship a 30-day window.
4. Audit cached or refreshed catalog state without inference.
5. Revalidate a preset on actual launch before applying defaults.
6. Withdraw only the recommendation when evidence changes; retain exact-model use.
7. Expand reviewed choices across OpenAI, Anthropic, Google-through-OpenRouter,
   xAI, Mistral, Kimi, GLM, DeepSeek, Qwen, and MiniMax.
8. Keep the table data-only and removable.
9. Do not add automatic successor selection, a universal sampler, or a ranking.
10. Leave native Google `sandpi gemini` distinct from deliberate OpenRouter Gemini.

## Evidence boundaries

Catalog presence and capability metadata do not establish coding quality. The
roles are not benchmark grades. The exact model-revision checks prove only that a
reviewed recommendation still targets the revision it named.

Synthetic tests establish schema, withdrawal, launch behavior, and Pi-side
reporting. They do not establish live provider performance. Real inference
qualification remains an explicit acceptance activity using named models and
operator authorization.

Historical community reports can motivate a test, but no such report is used here
to assert current behavior. The implementation decisions above rest on current
primary contracts and direct parent-revision comparisons.
