# Rev0044 research — handoff as a visible native workflow

Research date: 2026-10-08. Target: Pi 1.1.0, commit
`abe508e1b89912adde45528136c3221eb69acdd7`. Online primary-source inspection in this
turn, separate from the supplied archive and from execution evidence. No model
benchmark, live provider inference, or recommendation renewal was performed.

## What the starting archive established

Rev0043 provides a searchable model picker and a product-direction note proposing
a deliberate handoff and review ritual. Existing request wrappers cover ordinary
Pi turns, paid permission, cancellation and accounting. The host remains the room
manager, not a second conversation engine. This revision composes those facilities
rather than adding a side summarization client or an automatic context router.

## Native handoff exists as an example, not a reason to copy its whole loop

The pinned `examples/extensions/handoff.ts` serializes conversation context,
requests a completion, lets the user edit it, then calls native `newSession` with
`parentSession` and `withSession`. It confirms that focused transfer can use a
native replacement context and an unsent editor draft.

Sandpi instead uses an ordinary `/sandbrief` prompt for generation, then a local
`/sandhandoff` command for reviewing and transferring the existing answer. The
normal turn is visible and cancellable through Pi, uses the existing wrappers and
paid policy, and retains its answer in the source conversation. A second completion
and its extra loading/accounting lifecycle are unnecessary. This is a product
choice, not a claim that the upstream completion API bypasses Sandpi's wrappers.

Sources:
- https://raw.githubusercontent.com/earendil-works/pi/abe508e1b89912adde45528136c3221eb69acdd7/packages/coding-agent/examples/extensions/handoff.ts
- https://raw.githubusercontent.com/earendil-works/pi/abe508e1b89912adde45528136c3221eb69acdd7/packages/coding-agent/src/core/extensions/types.ts

## Native projection owns omissions, edits and compaction

`ReadonlySessionManager` exposes `buildSessionProjection`. Its provenance-preserving
entries pair raw source ownership with projected messages, which can be empty after
omission. The projection also applies the native compaction boundary and branch-local
context edits. Selecting from raw `getBranch` text could resurrect content Pi has
removed. Sandpi uses the projection and stops at an omitted/incomplete latest reply
instead of silently finding an older success.

The pinned source also documents that setup-only sessions are not persisted until
a user or assistant message exists. A new editor draft is not itself such a message;
the workflow therefore does not promise durable storage or recoverability for an
unsaved source. Native history and files remain Pi-owned.

Source:
- https://raw.githubusercontent.com/earendil-works/pi/abe508e1b89912adde45528136c3221eb69acdd7/packages/coding-agent/src/core/session-manager.ts

## Replacement contexts and terminal/UI differences matter

Pi invalidates the captured extension instance on session replacement/reload.
`newSession` supports `withSession`, whose supplied context is the valid location
for setting the replacement editor. The native handoff example uses that seam.
The interactive submission code also distinguishes leading `/` commands and `!`
shell input. Sandpi prefixes the reviewed body with a fixed ordinary instruction
so text copied from a reply cannot accidentally become a top-level command.

RPC supports editor and confirmation dialogs, but `getEditorText()` returns an
empty string regardless of the client's actual draft, and `set_editor_text` is
fire-and-forget. Therefore the new handoff transition is TUI-only. This is a
conservative Sandpi limitation, not a claim that RPC cannot create sessions.

Sources:
- https://raw.githubusercontent.com/earendil-works/pi/abe508e1b89912adde45528136c3221eb69acdd7/packages/coding-agent/src/core/agent-session.ts
- https://raw.githubusercontent.com/earendil-works/pi/abe508e1b89912adde45528136c3221eb69acdd7/packages/coding-agent/src/modes/interactive/interactive-mode.ts
- https://raw.githubusercontent.com/earendil-works/pi/abe508e1b89912adde45528136c3221eb69acdd7/packages/coding-agent/docs/rpc-extension-ui.md

## Prompt templates belong in native package resources

The pinned prompt-template guide describes Markdown frontmatter, optional argument
hints and `${@:-default}` expansion. Packages can declare exact prompt paths, and
native package filters can disable all prompts (`[]`) or select/exclude individual
resources. This is sufficient for a handoff-writing and evidence-review ritual.
There is no reason to inject those instructions into every turn or add tools.

Inspection of the supplied guest reconciliation showed that it replaced the core
package's object declaration with a plain source string. With newly bundled prompts,
that would undo a user's resource opt-out on the next launch. Rev0044 retains only
the existing native `prompts` field while keeping the mandatory provider extension.
No native prompt parser or resource-settings schema is reimplemented.

Sources:
- https://raw.githubusercontent.com/earendil-works/pi/abe508e1b89912adde45528136c3221eb69acdd7/packages/coding-agent/docs/prompt-templates.md
- https://raw.githubusercontent.com/earendil-works/pi/abe508e1b89912adde45528136c3221eb69acdd7/packages/coding-agent/docs/packages.md

