# Rev0079 research — 2026-10-09 / Lookout

This note records the external contracts used for local catalog-recency discovery.
It does not claim that recently listed models are better, ready, or recommended.

## OpenRouter Models API

Official Models documentation inspected 2026-10-09:

- https://openrouter.ai/docs/guides/overview/models
- https://openrouter.ai/api/v1/models

The documented model-object schema defines `created` as a numeric Unix timestamp
for when the model was added to OpenRouter. The documented server-side `newest`
sort means “most recently added to OpenRouter,” and models without data for a sort
dimension sort last. Omitting a sort preserves the default order.

The live text-model response inspected on the same date contained the `created`
field on newly listed entries. That establishes a usable catalog-added signal; it
does not establish original release chronology, endpoint readiness, or model
quality. Sandpi stores only a bounded integer projection and does not copy the
live descriptions, benchmarks, endpoint lists, or other unneeded fields into Pi.

The API documentation also distinguishes catalog/model metadata from endpoint
routing and pricing. Lookout therefore does not interpret a recent row as a live
endpoint probe or use recency to override price, capability, permission, or route
checks.

## Pinned Pi ownership

Pinned Pi remains 1.1.0 at commit
`abe508e1b89912adde45528136c3221eb69acdd7`:

- https://raw.githubusercontent.com/earendil-works/pi/abe508e1b89912adde45528136c3221eb69acdd7/packages/coding-agent/src/core/extensions/types.ts

The existing Sandpi picker receives exact registered native model objects and uses
Pi's model setter only after explicit confirmation. Lookout adds no Pi fork or
parallel model registry; it projects optional OpenRouter discovery metadata beside
the existing native object.

## Design conclusions

1. Call the field **catalog-added time**, never model release date.
2. Use the loaded snapshot's fetch time as the stable reference rather than a
   moving command-time clock.
3. Treat absent or malformed evidence as unknown, not old, new, or zero.
4. Keep default ordering unchanged; freshness is explicit through `--recent` or
   `--sort newest`.
5. Keep local discovery separate from refresh, authentication, provider readiness,
   paid permission, recommendation recipes, and native model selection.
