## Rev0024 cold runtime-upgrade research — 2026-10-06

### Current pinned releases

- Pi latest tagged release checked this turn: v1.0.4, exact commit
  `7c10bd4337495ee613f2224843ecdf349b80d1df`.
  `https://github.com/earendil-works/pi/releases/tag/v1.0.4`
- Pi sessions and context at the pinned tag:
  `https://github.com/earendil-works/pi/blob/v1.0.4/packages/coding-agent/docs/sessions.md`
- Pi packages at the pinned tag:
  `https://github.com/earendil-works/pi/blob/v1.0.4/packages/coding-agent/docs/packages.md`
- Cloud Hypervisor latest stable release checked this turn: v53.0.
  `https://github.com/cloud-hypervisor/cloud-hypervisor/releases/tag/v53.0`
- Cloud Hypervisor compatibility statement:
  `https://github.com/cloud-hypervisor/cloud-hypervisor/blob/v53.0/README.md`

Pi's native sessions, packages, extensions, MCP state and guest configuration are
ordinary files inside Sandpi's persistent guest disk. A cold whole-disk rollback
therefore rewinds them as opaque bytes; a retained forward disk can restore them
without Sandpi parsing Pi's session format. The live host workspace and canonical
credential bank are separate host objects and are not part of the disk switch.

Cloud Hypervisor explicitly does not support snapshot/restore across different
versions. Sandpi therefore does not make CH RAM/device snapshots its
cross-revision compatibility contract. The stable object is a stopped sparse raw
guest disk plus bounded exact host metadata. Cloud Hypervisor remains pinned to
53.0; the new transaction changes host revision handling, not the VMM/network or
guest authority boundary.

### Supplied Sandcodex 0.88.0 transaction precedent

- Exact source SHA-256:
  `08ce115cc4cb85dd728f1b44ef73157057d6b8147cc664b3ccdb611bd8537fb4`.
- `sc_state_dispatch()` begins at reference line 123076.

The useful ideas are a durable journal, exact preimages, expected-content
digests, staged atomic publication, explicit rollback and post-operation receipt.
Sandpi adapts those principles to one room-local runtime upgrade. It does not
adopt Sandcodex's portable-state document, Codex session/rollout representation,
provider translation or campaign machinery.

### Resulting design decisions

- A fixed runtime contract records only the immutable control-plane/guest pins
  that actually reached room readiness.
- Only a completed SSH room-identity command commits the successful contract;
  host metadata edits and process launch do not.
- One cold rollback slot is prepared by reflink or sparse copy and published by
  one directory rename after fsync.
- Rollback and inverse forward restoration are restartable disk-name state
  machines with exact source and target `room.json` preimages.
- Guest-local Pi state follows the whole disk. Workspace, credentials, host Nix
  store, VMM RAM/device state and host observations remain outside the slot.
- Destruction of either opposite disk requires an explicit confirmed accept or
  retry; failed target disks cannot be promoted through the forward path.


## Rev0025 portable/runtime convergence research — 2026-10-06

### Current immutable integration pins

- **Pi 1.0.4 release and source commit.**
  https://github.com/earendil-works/pi/releases/tag/v1.0.4
  https://github.com/earendil-works/pi/tree/7c10bd4337495ee613f2224843ecdf349b80d1df

  Pi remains the guest-native owner of sessions, packages, extensions, MCP,
  tools, models, and conversation trees. Portable Sandpi backup treats all of
  that state as opaque guest-disk content rather than introducing a second
  migration format.

- **Cloud Hypervisor 53.0 release and compatibility statement.**
  https://github.com/cloud-hypervisor/cloud-hypervisor/releases/tag/v53.0
  https://github.com/cloud-hypervisor/cloud-hypervisor/blob/v53.0/README.md

  Upstream does not guarantee snapshot/restore across VMM versions. Sandpi uses
  cold raw disks for both portability and runtime rollback, never a RAM/device
  snapshot as a durable cross-version contract.

### Nix portability and exact-set proof

- **Copy closures between stores and local binary caches.**
  https://nix.dev/manual/nix/2.28/command-ref/new-cli/nix3-copy
  https://nix.dev/manual/nix/2.28/store/types/local-binary-cache-store

- **Inspect store paths without building them.**
  https://nix.dev/manual/nix/2.28/command-ref/new-cli/nix3-path-info

- **Query closure requisites and create explicit roots.**
  https://nix.dev/manual/nix/2.28/command-ref/nix-store/query
  https://nix.dev/manual/nix/2.28/command-ref/nix-store/add-root

- **Realisation can build derivations.**
  https://nix.dev/manual/nix/2.28/command-ref/nix-store/realise

  These contracts drive the split between manifest and full-cache import.
  Manifest mode may fetch non-derivation objects through normal substituters but
  never realizes a `.drv` merely because it appears in a lease. Both modes use
  `nix path-info --stdin` to prove the complete exact set before publishing the
  room, then retain closure roots under the lifecycle lock.

### Resulting design decisions

- A portable bundle contains one active guest disk; a Lifeboat slot is local
  two-disk transition evidence and blocks backup until resolved.
- Backup v2 carries, but does not reinterpret, the source runtime contract.
- Legacy v1 imports as runtime-unknown and follows the cold first-boot path.
- Import never carries rollback/forward state and cannot become visible before
  exact Nix retention and no-replace room publication complete.
- Live project data, canonical credentials, and host control evidence remain
  separate backup domains.

